	"Comet Splinter": "A sliver of comet kept close to the heart, so the wounds her spells open smolder a while with the cold fire it lends them.",
	"Comet's Eye": "A polished eye of comet-ice: land a true strike and it recalls, briefly and violently, how it once met the ground.",
	"Cometweave Circlet": "Woven with comet-tail so her opening bolt falls like the night's first star: early, unbidden, already burning.",
	"Cometweave Handwraps": "Star-threaded across the palm, they leave a smoldering comet-streak in every wound that keeps burning after the bolt has passed.",
	"Cometweave Underleggings": "Star-threaded to reward commitment: blink in, and the cast that follows falls heavier for the ground she gave up.",
	"Common Weed": "Roadside green, plucked by the fistful. Every remedy starts here.",
	"Concordium Relic": "A shard of the Concord's seal-keepers, interred with the fifth binding. It hums near the blight.",
	"Covenant Crownplate": "Plate of the old covenant, stamped with the crownless sigil; struck down to its deepest footing, it turns the beating itself into banked holy charge.",
	"Cracked Bone": "Splintered and grey. The graves give up so many it's barely worth stooping for.",
	"Crownfall, the Kingdom's End": "The blade in the king's hands the moment he stopped being one; it has cut nothing smaller than a kingdom since, and every sweep still reaches that far.",
	"Crownspike, the Last Decree": "The king's final order was carried on a pike, not parchment — and every third stroke it still delivers the sentence, straight through whatever armor thought it could appeal.",
	"Crude Extract": "Boiled down in a field pot. Cloudy, sharp, unreliable.",
	"Cursed Idol|B-A": "A masterwork idol of a nameless god, its counsel honest to the point of ruin.",
	"Cursed Idol|D-C": "A serviceable cursed idol, a small god no scripture names, answering plainly enough to be dangerous.",
	"Cursed Idol|F-E": "A rough-whittled idol to no god in particular, muttering answers crooked enough to mislead as often as ruin.",
	"Cursed Idol|S": "A god with no name, carved from stone dug beneath the Ember Throne; ask it nothing and it still answers, truthfully, in wounds.",
	"Cutthroat Fury": "Favored by men who don't plan on being hit back.",
	"Dawn Coin": "A coin struck at first light and never spent, warm to the touch; whatever it marks goes on burning with a little of the dawn.",
	"Dawnfall, Hammer of the Final Oath": "The last oath comes down like the sun itself: Conviction's slam breaks the dark, and all it touches is left burning in a light it cannot walk out of.",
	"Dawnless Watch, Boots That Never Slept": "They kept a watch through a night that never broke into dawn; step aside from a blow and it leaves the striker marked to the dark, while the sleepless watch mends you for staying at your post.",
	"Dawnmend": "Legend says its maker mended a dying Crown-bearer at the first fall — and vanished before the thanks. Alchemists theorize it could be synthesized into something greater still.",
	"Dawnstone Gauntlets": "Weighted at the knuckle with dawnstone, so the plainest blow carries a fraction of the wearer's whole faith into it.",
	"Dawnstone Greathelm": "Set with a dawnstone that hoards the last of the day's mending against the dark still to come.",
	"Dawnstone Legguards": "Dawnstone in the knee holds a bowing line where it stands — though the same stubbornness lets only half the Holy mending through.",
	"Dead Road, Boots of Returning Kings": "The road every buried king walks back down; nothing on it holds him, and each hold he shrugs off returns a measure of the life the grave meant to keep.",
	"Deadflesh Rust": "The flesh it grows back isn't quite yours. It refuses second opinions.",
	"Deadstill Boots": "Weighted to hold a marksman dead still; every blow taken while planted only sets the feet deeper.",
	"Deathbed Bargain": "You walked away from that one. Mórwyn's ledger doesn't lose entries — it follows, and it collects.",
	"Deathwish Fury": "Two extra seconds of glory. The body keeps the ledger.",
	"Debtcollector": "It does not kill so much as foreclose — every crit on a cursed man is a payment collected, principal to the blade and interest back to the hand that swings it.",
	"Defective Elixir of Might": "Mostly pepper and spirits. The surge is real, if brief.",
	"Defective Elixir of Warding": "The skin prickles, and some blows land softer. Some.",
	"Defective Health Potion": "A first-year's brew that separates in the vial. Shake well and hope.",
	"Defective Health Tonic": "A beginner's simmer. The mending comes when it comes.",
	"Defective Mana Potion": "A beginner's blue. The spark arrives; the shine does not.",
	"Defective Mana Tonic": "A first-year's simmer, thin as rain. The mana returns; the confidence doesn't.",
	"Dockside Red": "No Accord stamp, no questions, no warranty on the ribs.",
	"Dogfight Fury": "Everything hits harder. Yes, everything.",
	"Dragonhide Duffel": "Scaled hide that shrugs off ember and edge alike — a drake died badly so this one seam would never wear through.",
	"Dreamer's Haze": "One swallow and the stars talk back. Your blade stops listening.",
	"Duelist's Blade|B-A": "A masterwork duelist's blade balanced on a fingertip, made for the knight who trusts a measured answer over a heavy one.",
	"Duelist's Blade|D-C": "A serviceable arming-sword for the paladin who fences his verdicts rather than crushing them, quick where the maul is patient.",
	"Duelist's Blade|F-E": "A thin blade cut down from a broken longsword — light, honest, and the sort of thing the crusade quietly disapproves of.",
	"Duelist's Blade|S": "An edge so fast the fair fight was never in doubt; it slips the blow and returns the judgment in a single breath, and the orders trust it least of all.",
	"Duelist's Knot|B-A": "A masterwork duelist's knot, tied by a hand that fakes high and cuts low and is never where the answer lands.",
	"Duelist's Knot|D-C": "A cord tied in the old duelist's fashion, worn by soldiers who learned the line's discipline and then learned to break it cleanly.",
	"Duelist's Knot|F-E": "A frayed cord tied wrong, the old duelist's knot half-remembered from a better teacher.",
	"Duelist's Knot|S": "The knot of a fencer the whole muster feared to spar; no lunge ever caught him, and every feint of his did.",
	"Dustbefore, Boots of the First Charge": "First off the line and always ahead of the dust; dodge a strike and the shield answers a moment sooner while the enemy is still open.",
	"Earthen Circlet|B-A": "A fine fillet fired glass-hard and veined with stone, an anchor for the archmage who will not be rushed.",
	"Earthen Circlet|D-C": "A standard earthen circlet of fired clay and riverstone, patient and plain, for the mage who means to last.",
	"Earthen Circlet|F-E": "A rough fillet of baked mud, cracked and shedding grit, but heavy enough to steady a nervous first cast.",
	"Earthen Circlet|S": "A circlet of first-fired clay that outlasts the hand that shaped it; she wears the world's own patience at her brow.",
	"Earthen Handwraps|B-A": "A masterwork pair of fired clay-thread wraps, weighting each cast so even a thrown bolt lands with heft.",
	"Earthen Handwraps|D-C": "Standard earthen wraps, clay-stiffened, lending thin wrists the patience of stone under a long cast.",
	"Earthen Handwraps|F-E": "Rough mud-caked wraps, cracking at the knuckle, but heavy enough to steady a shaking first cast.",
	"Earthen Handwraps|S": "Wraps of first-clay that never tire; her hands carry the weight of the ground into everything they throw.",
	"Earthen Robe|B-A": "A fine robe of fired clay-weave and packed silt, a wall she can wear and still cast through.",
	"Earthen Robe|D-C": "A standard earthen robe layered in clay-thread and river-silt, letting a battle-mage fight from inside her own nova.",
	"Earthen Robe|F-E": "A stiff mud-caked smock, cracking at the folds, that dresses her more like a scarecrow than a wall.",
	"Earthen Robe|S": "A robe of first-clay that will not break where she stands; the nova rages around her and she does not move.",
	"Earthen Underleggings|B-A": "A masterwork pair of packed clay-and-silt legguards, an anchor for the battle-mage who fights where she stands.",
	"Earthen Underleggings|D-C": "Standard earthen underleggings, weighted with fired clay at the knee, to hold the ground her frost freezes.",
	"Earthen Underleggings|F-E": "Rough clay-caked leg-wraps, cracking at the joint, that weigh her down more than they hold her up.",
	"Earthen Underleggings|S": "Underleggings of first-clay set into the earth; she holds her frozen ground the way a hill holds its place.",
	"Eclipse's Hem, Cloak of No Witness": "Hemmed in eclipse-dark, it eats the magic that reaches you — and the warding itself feeds the warmth in your blood.",
	"Elderwall, Ward of the Walking Wood": "A knot of the walking wood, the eldest forest that pulls up its own roots to move; spellfire breaks on it, and inside its shelter your breath keeps its own steady time.",
	"Emberforged Hold": "Bound in metal that fell burning the night the first Crown shattered; what you set inside it does not rot, does not cool, and does not leave until you reach for it.",
	"Empty Witness, Cowl Between Heartbeats": "There was a witness once; the cowl saw to that — spellfire breaks on it soft, and the warmth already stolen burns a heartbeat longer for the shrug.",
	"Empty Witness, Grips Between Heartbeats": "Hands that leave no print and no armor whole; each knife of the fan pulls its own thread loose.",
	"Empty Witness, Wraps Between Heartbeats": "No hold keeps these legs long; shake a chain and the dark opens again, Shadow Dash waiting ahead of schedule.",
	"End of Night": "The last knife of the dark hour; a true edge will not settle for one throat and goes hunting the nearest second.",
	"Endless Shade, Cord Around the Moon": "A cord long enough to loop the moon and pull the night over it; each blow it lets him dodge marks the fool who threw it and drags every live curse deeper into the dark.",
	"Equation Robe": "Worked over with a single unfinished equation, it turns aside a share of every spell as though the answer were already written.",
	"Event Horizon, Vestment of No Return": "Past its hem is a line nothing crosses back over; magic thrown at him falls in and never returns, and what it carried comes back to him as life.",
	"Eventide, Robe of the Last Constellation": "Sewn with the last constellation to set before the fall; commit to the cast and the light it carries comes down cold enough to still what it strikes.",
	"Ever Oath, Feet of the Faithful": "The ever-oath holds the faithful where they stand, and every chain that fails to move them is melted down into holy charge.",
	"Everbloom": "Mórwyn's rot refuses every ending. The Everbloom is the answer some nameless alchemist grew to refuse hers. Theory holds it could be synthesized into something greater.",
	"Everwild, Heart of the First Grove": "The living heart of the first grove the Concord ever fenced and never could tame; nothing given to the wild is lost, and everything you outlast is kept.",
	"Eye of the Tempest, Jerkin of Still Air": "At the storm's heart the air stands perfectly still; magefire breaks around this jerkin, and inside that calm your breathing never once falters.",
	"Far-Witness": "A bow built to answer a question the enemy never knew was asked; the farther the shot, the less their armor is worth.",
	"Faultscribe Robe": "Its clay is scribed over with the fault-lines of a breaking world, and like that world it cracks but will not fall — though the effort spends the frost that would have healed her.",
	"Faultstone Circlet": "Fired clay run through with one hairline fault; the blows it drinks it hoards, a drop at a time, against the moment she needs them back.",
	"Faultstone Handwraps": "Clay-caked wraps that lend each cast a little of the stone's weight, so even a thrown bolt lands with heft.",
	"Faultstone Underleggings": "Clay at the knee that plants her upright when she is all but spent, one last wall between her and the blow — bought against the nova's mending.",
	"Faultwalker": "Each step lands on ground already deciding to fail, so the blows meant to move him only pack the fault tighter under his heels.",
	"Featherweave Circlet|B-A": "A masterwork weave that seems to float a hair above the hair, so nothing at all drags at her thoughts.",
	"Featherweave Circlet|D-C": "A standard featherweave circlet, airy enough to forget, keeping the head cool and the casting quick.",
	"Featherweave Circlet|F-E": "A thin scavenged weave, light but loose, forever slipping down over one eye at the worst moment.",
	"Featherweave Circlet|S": "A circlet so weightless it might not be there, and her mind moves as though unburdened by a body at all.",
	"Featherweave Handwraps|B-A": "Finely spun wraps that barely touch the skin, the gestures pouring out clean and unbroken.",
	"Featherweave Handwraps|D-C": "Standard featherweave wraps, weightless at the wrist, letting the sigils flow faster than a bare hand can.",
	"Featherweave Handwraps|F-E": "Thin ravelling wraps, light but forever coming loose, so the sigils stutter as often as they flow.",
	"Featherweave Handwraps|S": "Wraps she cannot feel at all; her hands move at the speed of the intent, with nothing in between.",
	"Featherweave Robe|B-A": "A finely woven robe of near-nothing, following her through a blink as if it were part of the spell.",
	"Featherweave Robe|D-C": "A standard featherweave robe, so light the caster can vanish and return without her own hem slowing her.",
	"Featherweave Robe|F-E": "A gauzy scavenged shift, light but fragile, that flutters when she blinks and snags on everything.",
	"Featherweave Robe|S": "A robe that has forgotten it has weight; she and it are equally hard to pin to any one place.",
	"Featherweave Underleggings|B-A": "Finely woven leg-weave that follows a blink like a shadow, never a half-beat behind her feet.",
	"Featherweave Underleggings|D-C": "Standard featherweave underleggings, so light the wearer forgets her legs are clothed, which suits an instant blink.",
	"Featherweave Underleggings|F-E": "Thin scavenged leg-wraps, light but slack, that bunch and drag at exactly the wrong step.",
	"Featherweave Underleggings|S": "Underleggings of near-nothing; between one blink and the next there is no cloth to catch, only the going.",
	"Fervor, Steps of Unending Charge": "Fervor never lets the charge end; the first strike of every rush lands hard enough to knock the plea out of a man's mouth.",
	"Final Censure, Gauntlets of the Final Oath": "The final censure brands holy fire into every strike, and in the Retribution stance the fire burns down to the confession.",
	"Final Censure, Greathelm of the Final Oath": "The final censure opens every hearing hard, and thrown from the Retribution stance the first blow leaves the accused too rattled to plead.",
	"Final Censure, Legguards of the Final Oath": "The final censure puts the whole body behind the verdict, and when Conviction swaps its stance the dragged chains are hauled wider to catch the guilty at the edges.",
	"Final Curse Treads": "The tenth curse was saved for the soles; the empowered cast they carry lands harder and drives every standing hex a stage deeper into the bone.",
	"Final Mile, Boots Beyond the Shrine": "For the mile past the last shrine, where no help is coming; as the wall nears breaking, the road itself takes over the mending.",
	"Final Name, Mark of the Inevitable": "The last name on the list is already written; the opening strike lands as a verdict, and closing the account feeds the warmth.",
	"Fine Silk": "Thread so fine it whispers. The capital's seamsters hoard it.",
	"Fine Steel": "Blue-sheened and true — the bar a master reaches for without looking.",
	"Firmament, the Heaven-Bearing Staff": "It shoulders a corner of heaven, and heaven pays its keep — the power that runs through it never empties and never fully cools.",
	"First Arrow, Mark of the Empty Sky": "The mark of the very first arrow loosed into an empty sky, before there was anything to aim at; the perfect shot it grants also feeds the hunt that follows.",
	"First Beast, Bracers of the Last Gale": "The first beast's sinew still runs through them; your frame leans into every shot, and even the storm's countless arrows each carry a share of your strength.",
	"First Beast, Hood of the Last Gale": "Said to be cut from the first thing that ever hunted under this sky; wearing it, nothing you gain is wasted — even the breath you don't need is saved for later.",
	"First Beast, Leggings of the Last Gale": "The first beast survived every winter by refusing to fall; wear its legs and, cornered and bleeding, your feet answer faster than fear.",
	"First Bloom, Pendant Before Winter": "The first flower to open before the last winter, kept in amber; every scrap of life that would spill over it is saved instead, against the cold to come.",
	"First Dawn, Gauntlets of the Final Oath": "Every blow carries a first dawn's weight, and while the Aegis holds overhead the hands strike for twice the conviction.",
	"First Dawn, Greathelm of the Final Oath": "It keeps a first dawn under its crest; grace that would spill from the Holy stance is gathered into a shield instead of wasted on a man already whole.",
	"First Dawn, Legguards of the Final Oath": "As the wall bows and the light thins toward first dawn, the legguards refuse the last step back, and the Holy mend answers the danger by pouring twice as hard.",
	"First Feint": "A knot tied for the man who fakes high and cuts low; the blade he draws out leaves its owner a step behind.",
	"First Light, Edge of the Vigil": "The watchful are paid at dawn — every blow you slip re-arms the leap, and the judgment that follows comes down as the first light, and it does not miss.",
	"First Star's Tooth": "A fang of the first star to burn: its fire clings to every wound and eats deepest where a ward has already broken.",
	"First to Answer, Cord of Immediate Oath": "The cord of the oath sworn without pause, first to answer any alarm; slip a blow wearing it and the Aegis is already rising before you think to ask.",
	"Fleet Iron": "Iron cut light enough to move in; a strike that lands on it kicks the wearer into a duelist's step instead of a grave.",
	"Fletcher's Token|B-A": "A finely carved token of a master fletcher, whose arrows fly the way she saw them in her hands.",
	"Fletcher's Token|D-C": "A fletcher's serviceable maker's-mark in bone, a small standing promise the arrow flies as it was cut to.",
	"Fletcher's Token|F-E": "A splinter of a broken shaft kept for luck, the fletcher who made it long forgotten.",
	"Fletcher's Token|S": "The mark of a fletcher who never made a crooked shaft in her life; hold it and the arrow simply obeys.",
	"Foul Residue": "Scraped from something's gland. It reeks, and it works, a little.",
	"Foxfire String": "A string that hums when danger nears, the way a fox goes still before it bolts; slip the blow and it pays you back with twin arrows.",
	"Frayed Pouch": "A greasy drawstring bag gone soft with handling; whatever it first carried, the owner didn't live long enough to miss it.",
	"Frayed Scraps": "Rag-ends off a dead man's hem. Sewn together, they cover something.",
	"Fresh Herb": "Cut this morning, still holding its scent — the daily base of every brew.",
	"Furnace Blue": "Lights the veins like Petra's forge. Casters swear by it. And at it.",
	"Gale Script": "A rune of wind-script that tugs her a hair clear of the blow and leaves the striker turned wide open.",
	"Gale-Sewn Jack": "A hunter's jack sewn tight against the high-ridge wind; it turns aside a little spellfire the same way it turns the cold.",
	"Gapfinder's Mark": "A killer's tally notched for the seams between plates; a solid hit opens the wound the way a butcher opens a joint.",
	"Garnet|Lv1-3": "A dull garnet pebble, common as gravel in the Vale; still, armour set with even this rough chip holds together a breath longer than it ought.",
	"Garnet|Lv10": "Perfected and heavy as a shield-boss, it teaches flesh the trick metal already knows: to take the blow and simply remain.",
	"Garnet|Lv4-6": "Faceted and darkened to old-wine red, it lends the wearer the same stubbornness it shows — hard to chip, harder to break.",
	"Garnet|Lv7-9": "A deep, near-flawless stone that soaks up harm and keeps standing, the way the old levies were drilled to.",
	"Gate-Shrine Armor": "Iron from a shrine that stood in a gateway until the gate fell; it holds a failing line unmoved, though a body kept this rigid mends at half its pace.",
	"Gatebrow Gauntlets": "Iron heavy enough that a blocked blow is an invitation; now and then the hand answers before the sword can.",
	"Gatebrow Helm": "A brow-bar thick as a portcullis rail; the first killing blow to find it comes away only half the blow it meant to be.",
	"Gatebrow Legplates": "Planted, they gather weight from every blow struck against them; the longer the wall is beaten, the less each strike gets through.",
	"Gateheart": "A splinter of gate-timber that would not burn; it banks what mending you can spare against the next assault, at the cost of the slow healing you'd have leaned on.",
	"Ghostlight Charm|B-A": "A masterwork charm, its pale light showing the dead to the living without a flame to give them away.",
	"Ghostlight Charm|D-C": "A serviceable ghostlight, cold corpse-shine enough to pick the dark's edges out.",
	"Ghostlight Charm|F-E": "A pinch of grave-fungus in a cracked locket, its glow more guttering than guiding.",
	"Ghostlight Charm|S": "A light that knows the dead by name; it shows the wearer who has gone, and where the living should not stand.",
	"Giantsblood": "The Concord's wars buried things bigger than men, and somebody bottled one. Never brewed by human hands, the theory goes — and it could seed something greater.",
	"Gilded Weave": "Cloth shot through with drawn gold, light as breath and twice as dear.",
	"Glassfang Treads": "A glass fang of venom set in each heel — a Shadow Dash out of them lands with the green still on the stride.",
	"Glasswing|B-A": "Masterwork steel ground translucent at the edge; it cuts on the backswing you never saw begin.",
	"Glasswing|D-C": "A proper thin blade, quick in the wrist and quicker on the return.",
	"Glasswing|F-E": "A sliver of window-glass lashed to a stick — light, brittle, and gone before it can be caught.",
	"Glasswing|S": "Thinner than a moth's wing and just as silent — it has severed the thread before the light finds it.",
	"Gossamer Cloak|B-A": "Masterwork weave lighter than the air it displaces, gone from the line of a strike.",
	"Gossamer Cloak|D-C": "A serviceable gossamer cloak, light enough that the wearer moves before it does.",
	"Gossamer Cloak|F-E": "A gauze half-cloak, near weightless and near useless — except at not being where the blow falls.",
	"Gossamer Cloak|S": "So slight it barely exists; the whole garment is an argument for not being there when the blow arrives.",
	"Gossamer Cowl|B-A": "Masterwork spider-silk drawn to a haze, a face behind it the world will never quite place.",
	"Gossamer Cowl|D-C": "A serviceable gossamer hood, a breath of web the wearer soon forgets is there.",
	"Gossamer Cowl|F-E": "A scrap of veil-net, so thin it snags on stubble — but it weighs nothing and hides the eyes.",
	"Gossamer Cowl|S": "Spun finer than fog; the wearer forgets it entirely, and so does everyone who ever met them.",
	"Gossamer Grips|B-A": "Masterwork glovework drawn fine, all the guidance a needle needs and no more.",
	"Gossamer Grips|D-C": "Serviceable gossamer grips, light enough to read a heartbeat through the leather.",
	"Gossamer Grips|F-E": "Thin scavenged skin-gloves, half worn through — but a bare thumb feels a pulse just fine.",
	"Gossamer Grips|S": "So thin they are nearly not worn; the needle goes where the pulse says, exactly and once.",
	"Gossamer Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork weave that flows out of a strike's way and back before it lands.",
	"Gossamer Wraps|D-C": "Serviceable gossamer wraps, light enough to billow with the dodge and settle again.",
	"Gossamer Wraps|F-E": "Gauze leg-ties, so slight they tear on a bramble but never bind a stride.",
	"Gossamer Wraps|S": "So near weightless the leg forgets them; the dodge is already spent before the miss arrives.",
	"Grave Cowl|B-A": "Masterwork cerecloth, softened and rewoven, carrying the calm of the buried without the smell.",
	"Grave Cowl|D-C": "A serviceable shroud-cowl, cool at the brow and quiet at the ear.",
	"Grave Cowl|F-E": "A hood cut from grave-linen still stiff with the ground it came out of.",
	"Grave Cowl|S": "Cut from a shroud that already served one funeral; whoever wears it is halfway to not being here.",
	"Grave Grips|B-A": "Masterwork cerecloth wraps that keep the fingers still long past a living hand's nerve.",
	"Grave Grips|D-C": "Serviceable shroud-linen grips, steady where a living glove would shake.",
	"Grave Grips|F-E": "Grip-wraps of grave-cloth, cold and stiff, but they do not sweat and do not slip.",
	"Grave Grips|S": "Wound in grave-linen and cold to the bone; the fingers do not shake because the dead do not fear.",
	"Grave Treads|B-A": "Masterwork boots reworked from cerecloth and hide, calm on any ground.",
	"Grave Treads|D-C": "Serviceable grave-treads, the chill in the leather never fully gone.",
	"Grave Treads|F-E": "Boots pulled off the buried, stiff and cold, but stubborn past their years.",
	"Grave Treads|S": "They have walked out of more graves than into them, and the cold in them never thaws.",
	"Grave Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork cerecloth legwraps, calm in the gait as the long-buried.",
	"Grave Wraps|D-C": "Serviceable shroud-wraps, quiet and cold, wound the way a corpse is readied.",
	"Grave Wraps|F-E": "Burial-strips bound to the shin, stiff with the ground but grimly hard to wear out.",
	"Grave Wraps|S": "Wound like a body for the grave; nothing in the walk suggests the wearer means to stay upright.",
	"Gravebone Chausses": "Shinbones of the settled dead plated over his own, and the harder the field leans on him the deeper they set their heels.",
	"Gravebone Claws": "Knuckled in the finger-bones of a strangler; strike the hands that wear them and the dead man's grip strikes back on reflex.",
	"Gravebone Hood": "Skullcap and cowl of a debtor who died owing; it takes the worst of a crit the way its first owner took bad news — flatly, without breaking.",
	"Gravebound Boots|B-A": "Masterwork gravebound boots, the chain fine and cold, holding a man to the road long after the grave has called him.",
	"Gravebound Boots|D-C": "Serviceable gravebound boots, laced with grave-chain, walking their wearer well past his owed rest.",
	"Gravebound Boots|F-E": "Boots trailing a length of rusted grave-chain, keeping a man on his feet a while past when his rest fell due.",
	"Gravebound Boots|S": "Chained to graves that were never permitted to close; the dead in them walk on, and so, whether he wills it or not, does he.",
	"Graverobber's Mercy": "Half a resurrection at a quarter the price. The other half comes due.",
	"Gravesong": "A greatsword that hums low on the downswing like a funeral bell; it falls heavier than any blade its size, and takes its grim time doing it.",
	"Gravestone Hide": "Stand still long enough and the sexton starts his arithmetic.",
	"Greatstaff|B-A": "A masterwork greatstaff, its heartwood core wound in star-metal, calling the heavens down with real authority.",
	"Greatstaff|D-C": "A proper greatstaff, weighted for the long cast, that draws a measure of the sky down when she leans on it.",
	"Greatstaff|F-E": "A crude quarterstaff of banded iron, too heavy to be subtle and too plain to be more than a humming club.",
	"Greatstaff|S": "A staff that holds a corner of the firmament apart from the earth, and lets the whole weight fall where she points.",
	"Green Ruin, Root of the First Wild": "Cut from the root of the First Wild — the forest that stood before the Concord drew a single line; wound it once and it wakes, sprouting thorns on your arrows and grasping roots from the ground.",
	"Green Secret": "A secret kept in green — one drop worked into a wound eats the armor from the inside out.",
	"Green Silence, Shroud of the Hollow Grove": "Green as a grove the blight hollowed to silence; at the edge of dying it wraps close, and the blows that should end you mend you instead.",
	"Greenheart Idol|B-A": "A finely carved heartwood idol that hoards the forest's habit of surviving whatever is done to it.",
	"Greenheart Idol|D-C": "A serviceable greenheart idol, carved from wood still living, holding a little back against a hard hour.",
	"Greenheart Idol|F-E": "A rough whittling of green wood that keeps a little sap and a little luck.",
	"Greenheart Idol|S": "The green heart of a wood that will not stop growing; nothing it saves for her is ever wasted, and little it holds is ever spent.",
	"Greenstride": "Root-soled to keep her upright at the last, though what the ground gives her it takes back from the frost that heals.",
	"Greenwood Ghost, Hide of the Unseen Trail": "The greenwood keeps trails no eye has walked twice; slip a blow in this hide and the hunt's count moves on, as though the miss were a kill.",
	"Grimheart Staff|B-A": "A masterwork staff cored with a heart that will not stop, feeding him power drawn straight off his own pulse.",
	"Grimheart Staff|D-C": "A serviceable grimheart staff, its core a beast's heart cured to keep beating, strength trickling up the shaft from it.",
	"Grimheart Staff|F-E": "A crook of grave-oak with a rat's heart nailed to the head; it beats, faintly, and lends a little of its dying pulse.",
	"Grimheart Staff|S": "The heart at its crown was a man's once, and it remembers being one; every cast spends a beat of borrowed life it can never repay.",
	"Grimoire|B-A": "A master's ledger of borrowed power, gilt-edged and clasped, whose margins fill themselves in a hand no living scholar owns.",
	"Grimoire|D-C": "A journeyman's grimoire, columns ruled and clauses indexed, that reads back plainly and only rarely reads back something he never wrote.",
	"Grimoire|F-E": "A water-warped commonplace book, its pacts scrawled in a debtor's shaky hand; the terms are real, and the penalty for a misread word is his to keep.",
	"Grimoire|S": "Every page a signed instrument older than the Concord; he reads aloud, and something on the far side answers before he reaches the final word.",
	"Groundlock": "Weighted to lock a man to the field; near the end they become a floor he can't be driven below, though standing so still starves his recovery.",
	"Groundsplit, Cleats of the Falling Star": "They land like the falling star they're named for, hard enough to crack the ground; close on them and your roll is ready again before the shot is done.",
	"Gutter Haze": "The blue comes back on a slow smoke. So do you.",
	"Gutter Red": "Cut with blightwater in some Sable Court cellar. Puts the blood back; doesn't ask where it goes.",
	"Gutter Rust": "Old red gone brown. It mends the way rust holds a hinge.",
	"Gutterhide": "Thick as old boots and about as nimble.",
	"Hartshadow Leathers": "Leathers dark as a hart's shadow at dusk; a blow that should have landed finds only the space you have already left.",
	"Headsman's Mercy": "The kindest cut is the quick one; below a certain thread of life it does not wound so much as finish, and every finish leaves you warmer.",
	"Heart of the Wall|B-A": "A masterwork-set keystone shard, banded in iron; the fortress it came from is rubble, and the promise in it is not.",
	"Heart of the Wall|D-C": "A chip of fortress keystone kept as a promise: the wall fell, but the man who held it did not have to.",
	"Heart of the Wall|F-E": "A loose chip of common masonry a soldier keeps because it was all the wall he could carry.",
	"Heart of the Wall|S": "The heart-stone of a citadel that never fell, carried out by the last defender so its stubbornness would not die with the walls.",
	"Heartcage|B-A": "A masterwork heartcage, the caged heart beating strong and slow, a reserve of self he can spend at need.",
	"Heartcage|D-C": "A serviceable heartcage holding a heart still faintly beating — his, kept where he can draw on it.",
	"Heartcage|F-E": "A dented iron cage the size of a fist, a small stilled heart within it kept for spending later.",
	"Heartcage|S": "The last heart, beating on in the dark of its cage long after the body quit; he keeps it close, and spends it against the end.",
	"Heartsblood": "The Archive says no such brew exists. The Guard's last quartermaster swore one vial rode to the first fall in a Crown-bearer's saddlebag. Alchemists theorize it could be synthesized into something greater.",
	"Heartscorch Blue": "The full charge, paid from the chest. The Cinderborn call that honest arithmetic.",
	"Hexblade|B-A": "A masterwork hexblade layered with nested clauses, so a single stroke invokes three bargains at once.",
	"Hexblade|D-C": "An issue hexblade, its single clause cleanly graven, every edge a term that opens what it swears to open.",
	"Hexblade|F-E": "A notched fencing blade with one curse etched crooked down the fuller; it collects, but takes its cut of the wielder too.",
	"Hexblade|S": "Not sharpened but sworn to across a lifetime of signings; the edge is the whole contract, and it forecloses on whatever it touches.",
	"Hexcarved Treads|B-A": "Masterwork treads carved with nine curses, laying a spoiled road in his wake with every stride.",
	"Hexcarved Treads|D-C": "Serviceable hexcarved treads, curses cut one to each sole, so every road he walks is quietly ruined behind him.",
	"Hexcarved Treads|F-E": "Worn soles with a curse or two scratched in crooked, souring the road a step behind him.",
	"Hexcarved Treads|S": "Nine curses to the soles and a tenth held in reserve; the ground he leaves is condemned, and it knows to whom.",
	"Hexplate Circlet": "Runed to soften the first blow that finds the head, keeping the thought behind it clear enough to answer the second.",
	"Hexplate Handwraps": "Rune-plated across the knuckles, so a hand laid on her is answered with a snap of arcane force she never had to aim.",
	"Hexplate Underleggings": "Rune-plated at the shin, they gather weight from every blow taken, planting her heavier with each hit.",
	"Hexwalker Soles": "Soles cut with an old road-ward, so the snares of the blighted paths lose a little of their hold.",
	"Hexwall Cassock": "A cassock of layered hex-cloth that settles heavier with each blow it soaks, until she stands like a wall inside her own robe.",
	"High Hunt, Prowlers Above the Moon": "The hunt runs the rooftops where the moon is the only witness; the counter these treads throw drinks as it cuts.",
	"Hollow Finger": "A hollow finger-bone that holds what it is given; warmth stolen past full pools inside it — though the hand that carries it vanishes the slower.",
	"Hollow Step": "The foot goes down through a hollow the eye can't find, and the swing that chases it lands its owner marked for the miss.",
	"Hollowbone Red": "Fills the wound and hollows the bone. The Choir calls that a fair trade. The Choir would.",
	"Holy City, Bulwark of the Walking Cathedral": "A whole holy city carried on one man's back, and when its walls are nearly breached the light within does not dim — it doubles.",
	"Horizonless, Boots That Outran the Gale": "The huntress who wore them outran a storm to the world's edge and kept going; slip a blow in them and the hunt quickens as though a kill were counted.",
	"Horned Moon, Totem of the Old Hunt": "The horned moon under which the old hunts were called; the first strike falls hardest on prey already laid bare.",
	"Hornsong": "Bound with the horn of a beast that called its whole pack on one note; the volley it sings is wider, and gathers as slow as a breath drawn deep.",
	"House of Light, Reliquary Without Doors": "A whole house of light with no door to shut, so nothing gets in to be feared, and every blow that tries is turned to holy charge at the threshold.",
	"Hunter's Bracers|B-A": "Masterwork guards calloused into the exact shape of ten thousand loosings, quick and merciless.",
	"Hunter's Bracers|D-C": "Serviceable hunter's bracers, worn in along the draw-fingers to the shape of the release.",
	"Hunter's Bracers|F-E": "Stiff new leather that has not learned the draw yet and blisters the fingers teaching itself.",
	"Hunter's Bracers|S": "Bracers worn so deep into the draw that every arrow off them leaves a wound that keeps working after.",
	"Hunter's Harness|B-A": "A masterwork harness balanced for the closing stalk, every buckle set so nothing shifts or sings.",
	"Hunter's Harness|D-C": "A serviceable hunter's harness, rigged to carry a full quiver and nothing that betrays the approach.",
	"Hunter's Harness|F-E": "A makeshift harness of straps that rattles at a run and snags on every branch.",
	"Hunter's Harness|S": "A harness built for the last thirty paces of a hunt; she crosses open ground on it and the prey hears nothing until it is late.",
	"Hunter's Hood|B-A": "Masterwork leather blinkered just so, the whole field narrowed to the single thing worth loosing at.",
	"Hunter's Hood|D-C": "A serviceable hunter's hood that holds one line of sight steady while the rest of the world falls away.",
	"Hunter's Hood|F-E": "A rough hood cut low over the eyes, crude enough that it blinds nearly as much as it focuses.",
	"Hunter's Hood|S": "A hood that teaches the eye to choose before the hand moves; the first target is decided the moment she pulls it low.",
	"Hunter's Leggings|B-A": "Masterwork leggings that carry the closing run and plant it clean into a steady shooting stance.",
	"Hunter's Leggings|D-C": "Serviceable hunter's leggings, built to close thirty paces fast and then stop dead for the loose.",
	"Hunter's Leggings|F-E": "Rough breeches that manage the sprint but never quite the stop; you slide past the shot.",
	"Hunter's Leggings|S": "Legs that cross the killing ground at a dead run and stop as if rooted, the arrow already leaving the string.",
	"Hunter's Totem|B-A": "A finely bound totem of the season's first blood, sharpening the eye before the string is touched.",
	"Hunter's Totem|D-C": "A hunter's totem of first-kill bone; the hunt begins the moment the hand closes on it.",
	"Hunter's Totem|F-E": "A rough-strung tooth from an early kill, luck more hoped-for than proven.",
	"Hunter's Totem|S": "A relic of a first kill from a hunt older than the kingdom; grip it and the opening shot is half-decided already.",
	"Hunting Bow|B-A": "A finely balanced hunting bow that comes up like an extension of the arm, fast and unfailingly light.",
	"Hunting Bow|D-C": "A serviceable hunter's bow, quick to the draw and quicker to forgive a rushed release.",
	"Hunting Bow|F-E": "A boy's first bow re-strung one knot too many times, honest about killing rabbits and little else.",
	"Hunting Bow|S": "The bow she learned on, remade past perfection — quicker than thought, and it has never once lied to her about the shot.",
	"Hushbone": "Cut from the throat-bone of a thing that died mid-word, so the blow that whistles past feeds the silence and the next bolt is spoken twice.",
	"Hushshade Chausses": "Smoke below the belt, harder to hit and quicker to be elsewhere — but a body that keeps slipping never settles to drink deep, and Soulthirst goes a touch thirsty.",
	"Hushshade Claws": "Fingers that reach through the dark to where a thing truly is, not where it stands, so now and then a bolt loosed from them cannot miss.",
	"Hushshade Hood": "The dark inside it reads a swing before the arm commits, and marks the fool who threw it open for the reply.",
	"Hushveil Cowl": "It swallows sound and spellfire alike, so the first hymn to reach you arrives softened and too late to matter.",
	"Hushveil Grips": "They pick a seam as quiet as a lock — each hit frays the armor a thread wider.",
	"Hushveil Wraps": "They shrug off half of any chain or frost — though the cold taken in the bargain closes your own wounds slower.",
	"Ink of Teeth": "Its ink was rendered from something with a mouth and never truly dried, so the words he casts keep chewing after they land.",
	"Iron Promise": "A soldier's iron token, sworn upon; the promise turns a caster's fire the way a shield turns a blade.",
	"Ironmonger's Hide": "Skin like shop shutters. Moves like them too.",
	"Ironroot Sabatons": "Plate boots that take root where they stop; each blow you weather sets them deeper.",
	"Ironsole Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork ironsoles, a smith's heft under the step with none of its honest noise.",
	"Ironsole Wraps|D-C": "Issue ironsole wraps, plate beneath and felt above for weight the floor does not hear.",
	"Ironsole Wraps|F-E": "Scrap iron lashed under the sole and muffled in rag — heavy, and only half quiet.",
	"Ironsole Wraps|S": "Iron that carries the weight of a boot and none of its sound; the floor never learns it was crossed.",
	"Ironwall Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork guard-gauntlets, plated so a backhand lands like a mace and the grip never suffers for it.",
	"Ironwall Gauntlets|D-C": "Articulated iron heavy enough to make a fist a weapon in its own right — a counter you can throw when the sword's out of line.",
	"Ironwall Gauntlets|F-E": "Crude iron mitts heavy enough to bruise the wearer's own knuckles.",
	"Ironwall Gauntlets|S": "The gauntlets of a man who answered every blocked blow with his own hand; the counter came faster than the enemy's second swing.",
	"Ironwall Helm|B-A": "A masterwork greathelm built to eat the killing blow and give back only half of it.",
	"Ironwall Helm|D-C": "A plain guard-helm with a nasal thick as a gate-bar; it turns a mace-blow into a headache and calls that a fair trade.",
	"Ironwall Helm|F-E": "A blunt scrap-iron bucket with a bent nasal, riveted by someone in a hurry.",
	"Ironwall Helm|S": "A helm that has been struck by everything and dented by nothing; the hammer that could crush it was never forged.",
	"Ironwall Legplates|B-A": "Masterwork ironwall legplates, braced so the knee never buckles under a blow meant to fold it.",
	"Ironwall Legplates|D-C": "Leg-harness built for holding ground and nothing else — you do not run in these, and that is the whole promise.",
	"Ironwall Legplates|F-E": "Heavy scrap greaves bolted stiff, made for standing because running in them is hopeless anyway.",
	"Ironwall Legplates|S": "The legs of the last man on a wall that would not break; they did not step back because they could not conceive of it.",
	"Ironwall Plate|B-A": "A masterwork ironwall cuirass, every plate proofed and doubled; it soaks the blow and lets nothing behind it feel a thing.",
	"Ironwall Plate|D-C": "Plain, unlovely field-plate; it was made to be hit, and it is very good at being hit.",
	"Ironwall Plate|F-E": "Rough scrap-plate, thick where it doesn't need to be and thin where it does.",
	"Ironwall Plate|S": "A cuirass that has stood a full assault and shows only the dents to prove it; the man in it does not remember being pushed back.",
	"Ironwood Witness, Bracers of the Last Gale": "Grown, not forged; whoever closes to arm's reach eats a point-blank shaft and the wind behind it, and is put back where they belong.",
	"Ironwood Witness, Hood of the Last Gale": "Ironwood grown around an old wound until the wound became the strength; the blow it blunts marks the one who threw it, laid open for the shot to come.",
	"Ironwood Witness, Leggings of the Last Gale": "Ironwood set at the knee that has watched a hundred sieges and moved for none; hold the line long enough and your second wind comes quicker.",
	"Jade|Lv1-3": "A rough green pebble, cool and slick and hard to keep hold of; the wearer picks up a little of the same slipperiness.",
	"Jade|Lv10": "Perfected to a flawless green the eye slides off; a blow aimed true still somehow finds nothing there to hit.",
	"Jade|Lv4-6": "Polished smooth as a river-stone, it lends the body the trick of not quite being where the blow arrives.",
	"Jade|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless and cold to the touch, and strikes meant for the wearer keep closing on empty air.",
	"Journeyman's Elixir of Might": "Standard issue when the Ember Guard still held a line worth breaking.",
	"Journeyman's Elixir of Warding": "Reliable proofing for the caravans that still run the Emberward road.",
	"Journeyman's Health Potion": "The Ember Guard marched on this recipe. Half the Vale still carries one, years after the Guard stopped marching.",
	"Journeyman's Health Tonic": "The campfire standard on the Emberward road — poured after the fight, finished before the next.",
	"Journeyman's Mana Potion": "A journeyman's answer to an empty hand. The Wayfinders buy them by the crate.",
	"Journeyman's Mana Tonic": "Steady work by a journeyman's hand. The blue runs clear as the Vale's wells before the blight.",
	"Judgment Sigil|B-A": "A finely-cut sigil that reads a false faith like a forged document, unpicking the ward thread by thread as it passes sentence.",
	"Judgment Sigil|D-C": "A standard judgment sigil, the arbiter's seal, and a warded defence starts to come apart the moment it is stamped guilty.",
	"Judgment Sigil|F-E": "A crude seal cut from horn, the arbiter's mark pressed into every verdict he could reach; the guilty learn its shape early.",
	"Judgment Sigil|S": "The seal of a verdict none has overturned; no ward it is pressed against keeps its weave, for the sigil knows guilt has no right to armour.",
	"Kestrel Steps": "Light as a hovering hawk; step aside on them and the gap in the enemy's guard opens plain to the eye.",
	"Killer's Mark|B-A": "A masterwork tally, each name cut clean; the hand that carries it never needs telling who is next.",
	"Killer's Mark|D-C": "A serviceable bone token, one notch a name, that steadies the hand that keeps it.",
	"Killer's Mark|F-E": "A cracked knucklebone scratched with a few tally-marks, kept in a pocket like a bad habit.",
	"Killer's Mark|S": "A bone read like a ledger of the dead; the next name is already there, and the mark makes it so.",
	"Knife-Shadow Cloak": "The colour of a knife's own shadow, it turns the edge of spellfire aside before the light can bite.",
	"Knight's Rucksack": "Oiled canvas on iron buckles, cut for a rider who expected to be a long way from any friendly wall for a long while.",
	"Knotted Debt": "A cord of small debts, each knot a blow owed back; strike its bearer and one loop comes due at once.",
	"Lance|B-A": "A masterwork lance balanced so the prayer and the thrust arrive together, faith running down the shaft ahead of the point.",
	"Lance|D-C": "Issue lance of the watch-house, straight enough to make the charge before the man closes and settle it before he speaks.",
	"Lance|F-E": "A fence-rail lashed to a spearhead; it keeps the accused at arm's length, which is all an arbiter with no proper reach can ask.",
	"Lance|S": "A reach so true the verdict lands the instant it is aimed; the accused is judged at distance and never learns how close he came to a hearing.",
	"Lantern for None": "A lantern lit for no living eye; slip a blow and its cold light picks the swinger out of the dark.",
	"Lapis|Lv1-3": "A rough blue lump, the sort priests once crushed into ward-paint; even uncut it makes a spell hesitate at the skin.",
	"Lapis|Lv10": "Perfected to the blue the Concord's oldest seals were painted in; a curse arriving at the wearer finds the door already shut.",
	"Lapis|Lv4-6": "Cut and burnished, it turns a hostile working aside the way a good ward-wall does — quietly, before you feel the heat.",
	"Lapis|Lv7-9": "Deep near-flawless blue, and magic breaks across it like surf on a sea-wall, spent before it reaches the one behind.",
	"Last Dose, Boots of the Perfect Poison": "The last dose is the perfect one: the dash they carry lands empowered, and the road back to it is shorter.",
	"Last Heart, Cage That Beats in Darkness": "The final heart, still beating in its cage long after the body quit; every drop his healing wastes it saves, and after a Pact it hoards at double — a wall of blood held in the dark against the end.",
	"Last Hunt, Bracers of the Last Gale": "Every kill of the last hunt is worn into the leather; prey already laid open bleeds twice as freely for arrows off these.",
	"Last Hunt, Hood of the Last Gale": "The hood she wore on the hunt that outlived the kingdom, where the first arrow of any chase simply did not miss.",
	"Last Hunt, Leggings of the Last Gale": "The legs that ran down the last quarry the old world ever loosed at; close the gap in these and the volley carries one more arrow than it should.",
	"Last Measure, Seal of Perfect Justice": "The seal of a justice too perfect for this world; its measure bores straight through any ward, on the reasoning that guilt has no right to armour.",
	"Last Measure, Token of the Perfect Cut": "Carried by the veteran who measured every kill to the inch; in full fury the cut runs deeper than measure allows and keeps on bleeding.",
	"Last Pulse, Chausses Beneath All Names": "On the last heartbeat before he falls they lock the knees and open the terms: down there the Pact takes no blood at all, because there is none left to take.",
	"Last Pulse, Claws Beneath All Names": "Each fingertip beats faintly in time with his heart, and in the surge after a Pact that pulse doubles, and so does the weight behind every hand he lays on.",
	"Last Pulse, Hood Beneath All Names": "It keeps the head clear on the last heartbeat before collapse, hoarding every drop the Pact's surge would waste into a held wall of blood.",
	"Last Pulse, Vestment of the Final Debt": "The final line of the ledger, worn against the skin; near death it holds him standing and charges each blow the enemy lands straight to their own account, in blood.",
	"Last Rampart, Armor That Would Not Fall": "The cuirass off the last man on the last wall; braced to its limit, the blow it turns rebounds hard enough to stagger the one who threw it.",
	"Last Shadow, Cowl Between Heartbeats": "The last thing an unwounded man sees is its shadow — the first cut lands ruinous, and the stolen warmth floods in at once.",
	"Last Shadow, Grips Between Heartbeats": "The bleed they leave runs deeper on the marked — a body already promised to the dark bleeds toward it faster.",
	"Last Shadow, Wrap of the Absent Hand": "The absent hand strikes first and unseen — the opener no one watched for lands ruinous, and the warmth of it floods in.",
	"Last Shadow, Wraps Between Heartbeats": "Everything they hold is aimed ahead — a Fan of Knives thrown out of the dash forgets its spread and hunts one throat.",
	"Last Templar, Gauntlets of the Final Oath": "The last templar let no blow go unanswered; each counter falls as a smite, and the smiting mends the hand that gave it.",
	"Last Templar, Greathelm of the Final Oath": "The last templar's helm shrugs the fiercest blow, and banks the arrogance of it as holy charge to spend on the reply.",
	"Last Templar, Legguards of the Final Oath": "The last templar's stance only hardens under fire, and at its deepest the Aegis raised above it refuses to fall on schedule.",
	"Last Trail, Boots at the World's Edge": "Worn to the world's edge and past where the maps run out; cornered with nowhere left to run, they only make your feet quicker.",
	"Levitation Slippers|B-A": "A fine pair of slippers that float her true and steady, the drift finally hers to command and not the air's.",
	"Levitation Slippers|D-C": "Serviceable levitation slippers, holding the wearer a finger's breadth up, restful until the wind decides where she goes.",
	"Levitation Slippers|F-E": "Scuffed slippers that lift her a hair off the floor and then let the smallest draft push her about.",
	"Levitation Slippers|S": "Slippers that have quietly given up on the ground; she goes where she wills and gravity is not consulted.",
	"Lifebloom Pendant|B-A": "A masterwork pendant whose sealed bloom answers every frost with a surge of stubborn green.",
	"Lifebloom Pendant|D-C": "A standard lifebloom pendant, a seed of green sealed in amber, the battle-mage's small argument against dying.",
	"Lifebloom Pendant|F-E": "A withered seed clouded in cheap resin, its green nearly spent, answering frost with a weak flush and no more.",
	"Lifebloom Pendant|S": "A pendant holding a seed that will not die; where her frost falls, life insists on rising, and so does she.",
	"Longbow|B-A": "A masterwork stave that lays the horizon inside comfortable range, its cast flat as a drawn thread.",
	"Longbow|D-C": "A clean-grained longbow cut to a standard span; reach enough to end most questions before they are spoken.",
	"Longbow|F-E": "A crooked self-bow that throws far and true only if you forgive where it pulls left.",
	"Longbow|S": "Reach past reason — an arrow off it arrives before the sound of the string, and the prey never learns which ridge it came from.",
	"Lotus Haze": "The Rot-Chapel burns something like this and calls it worship.",
	"Marchbreaker": "It broke the enemy's march at the ford and never quite stopped swinging; in a red rage its cuts throw a second, ghostlit edge a beat behind the first.",
	"Marksman's Stance|B-A": "Masterwork stance-boots balanced to plant the whole body still enough to thread a distant eye.",
	"Marksman's Stance|D-C": "Issue marksman's boots, weighted low and wide to argue for standing your ground.",
	"Marksman's Stance|F-E": "Clumsy weighted boots that hold you still mostly by being too heavy to lift in a hurry.",
	"Marksman's Stance|S": "Boots that make of any patch of ground a shooting platform; once set, she and the earth are the same steady thing.",
	"Martyrfire Harness": "The martyrfire never goes out — every blow brands the guilty, and in the Retribution stance the brand burns clean to the marrow.",
	"Master's Draught of Renewal": "A master's life-work in a flask. Drink it at death's door.",
	"Master's Elixir of Might": "A master's blend that turns fear into forward motion. The Concord's shield-walls drank it by the barrel.",
	"Master's Elixir of Warding": "A master's proofing, steel-gray and bitter. The seal-keepers drank it before every descent.",
	"Master's Health Potion": "A master's reduction, twice distilled. It finds the deep wounds first.",
	"Master's Health Tonic": "A master's infusion. The flesh remembers how it was before the blight taught it otherwise.",
	"Master's Mana Potion": "A master's charge in a bottle. The air itches around it, the way it once did around the Guard's storm-line.",
	"Master's Mana Tonic": "A master's slow-brew. The mana returns like a tide answering a patient moon.",
	"Mastercrafted Alloy": "Alloyed to a recipe two generations refined and one smith died guarding.",
	"Masterwork Draught of Renewal": "Priced like a Crown Plaza address, because it has rebuilt more than one bloodline.",
	"Masterwork Elixir of Might": "Strength a master will sign for. Nothing borrowed, nothing owed.",
	"Masterwork Elixir of Warding": "The Accord's best answer to a falling mountain.",
	"Masterwork Health Potion": "The finest red an alchemist can sign. The Grand Archive ledgers every vial ever sold; the page is short.",
	"Masterwork Health Tonic": "Wounds close like evening flowers. A master signs perhaps ten a year, and the Sable Court bids on all of them.",
	"Masterwork Mana Potion": "Bottled lightning that agreed to wait. Masters sign few; the Archive counts fewer.",
	"Masterwork Mana Tonic": "Alive with light. A master makes perhaps a dozen in a career; the Wayfinders hold standing orders for ten.",
	"Mercy in Measure": "A measured step earns a measured answer: slip the blow and the next judgment falls with a little more weight, and a little mending owed back.",
	"Millstone Hide": "Grinds every blow to flour. Grinds you slow to match.",
	"Mirror Ward": "A palm-crystal that throws back a portion of the first blow's force, softening whatever lands.",
	"Moonclaw Pelt": "Pelt of a beast that hunted by the moon and healed by it alone; it carries you through the wound that should end you, but will not let you catch a second wind under the sun.",
	"Moonturn, Bow of Returning Night": "Bent from a bough that only fruited by moonlight; loose the storm, and like the night it is named for, it comes around again on its own.",
	"Moonweave": "Spun, they say, on a loom that only turns by moonlight. No needle has ever torn it.",
	"Mothknife": "Dust falls from the wing where a blow passed through empty air, and whatever breathes it slows.",
	"Mothshade Robe": "Threadbare as a moth's wing and just as hard to catch; a blow that lands only teaches the robe to be elsewhere the moment after.",
	"Mothsilk Cowl": "Woven of moth-silk that clings to whoever brushes it — slip a blow and the swinger wears your mark a moment.",
	"Mothsilk Grips": "Light enough to steady the needle; every eighth Stab finds its gap no matter how the prey twists.",
	"Mothsilk Wraps": "So slight a struck body rolls with the blow into a slip — but the wearer's knack for vanishing recovers slower for the trick.",
	"Mothstep": "Steps light as a moth on glass; a blow that catches you shakes loose into a slip.",
	"Mountain's Burden": "So much plate it slows the very blood in a man; worn low it becomes a standing floor no blow sinks past, though the weight starves his second wind.",
	"Mountainheart Gauntlets": "Slab-knuckled and dense; the bigger the man behind them, the more every blow simply weighs.",
	"Mountainheart Helm": "Cut from ore veined like a heartline; the mending you don't need it banks against the blows still to come.",
	"Mountainheart Legplates": "Ore-veined and immovable; wounded low, they set into a floor no blow drops beneath — though a body this planted mends slowly.",
	"Mule's Hide": "You'll take the hit fine. Catching the next one is your problem.",
	"Nameless God, Idol Beneath the Throne": "The idol of a god with no name, dug out from under the Ember Throne itself; the rot it seeds bites deepest in the already-cursed, as if it recognized its own faithful.",
	"Needleheel": "Heels honed to a point that bites the ground on the plant; step in and the shot rides the whole weight of the step.",
	"Never Broken, Cord of the First Betrayal": "The first betrayal was never forgiven and this cord never broke: every blow struck against you is answered, and the answer drinks.",
	"Never Forsworn, Seal That Binds the Dawn": "The one seal never once forsworn, binding the dawn itself to the oath; in the Holy stance the grace it keeps fills twice as fast, spilling into a shield rather than out of the world.",
	"Night Knot": "A single knot of dark tied at the throat; slip a blow with it worn and the striker is left standing in the open, marked.",
	"Nightsilk Cowl|B-A": "Masterwork nightsilk, the black so even it shows the eyes only for as long as it takes.",
	"Nightsilk Cowl|D-C": "An issue nightsilk hood, close-cut to show nothing but the eyes.",
	"Nightsilk Cowl|F-E": "Off-cut lining dyed lamp-black, uneven at the hem but dark where it counts.",
	"Nightsilk Cowl|S": "Silk that swallows the last glint; even the eyes surface only at the moment of the cut.",
	"Nightsilk Grips|B-A": "Masterwork grips that loose the blade before the eye admits the hand had one.",
	"Nightsilk Grips|D-C": "Issue nightsilk grips, cut so the knife leaves the hand clean.",
	"Nightsilk Grips|F-E": "Dark wrist-wraps, loose at the seam but quick to let a blade slip free.",
	"Nightsilk Grips|S": "The knife is thrown and gone while the witness is still deciding the hand was empty.",
	"Nightsilk Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork legwraps that put every thread into the forward step and waste none standing still.",
	"Nightsilk Wraps|D-C": "Issue nightsilk wraps, tailored so all the give runs toward the lunge.",
	"Nightsilk Wraps|F-E": "Dark shin-strips cut narrow, worn but always giving forward, never fighting the step.",
	"Nightsilk Wraps|S": "Cut for one direction only; the wearer springs, and nothing about the cloth stayed behind.",
	"Nightsilk Wrap|B-A": "A masterwork wrap, seamless and matte, that never once flashes in a passing torch.",
	"Nightsilk Wrap|D-C": "An issue nightsilk wrap, cut long and snagless for work that runs and does not stay.",
	"Nightsilk Wrap|F-E": "A length of black cloth wound close, frayed at the ends but with nothing on it to catch a hand.",
	"Nightsilk Wrap|S": "One perfect black length and nothing to snag — the wardrobe of a knife gone before the body folds.",
	"Nightwatch Steps": "Made for the nightwatch, quiet enough to slip a blow and leave the man who threw it wide open for the finishing.",
	"Nine-Hex Boots": "Nine curses carved one to a nail-hole, so a cast he commits through them strides in already half-condemned.",
	"Nine-Seal Mantle": "Nine warding seals stitched down the collar, one for each way a blade comes in; every blow it turns sets the next seal harder.",
	"Ninth Star, Circlet Beyond the Firmament": "The unclosing ninth eye reads the field before the fight does, and the first bolt it aims already knows where the ward runs thin.",
	"Ninth Star, Handwraps Beyond the Firmament": "The ninth star's fire clings to whatever she strikes, and bites deepest into the enemy whose ward has already been cracked open.",
	"Ninth Star, Underleggings Beyond the Firmament": "Commit the blink and the spell after lands like a falling star, and a nova thrown on its heels roots everything the cold touches.",
	"No Entry, Shoes of the Uncrossed Line": "They stand on a line nothing may cross; what would drag her from it is refused, and refusing it returns her the mana it cost.",
	"No Footfall, Shoes of the Empty Room": "They leave no footfall and the room stays empty of you — each blow slipped feeds the warmth already in your blood.",
	"No Horizon, Hands of the Crownless Host": "Steady where lesser hands shook; the sure cut they deliver can't be dodged, and it throws the man it finds clean off his feet.",
	"No Horizon, Helm of the Crownless Host": "They ran until the enemy had nowhere left to sight them from; slip a blow in it and the miss lays the foe bare while your own storm circles back.",
	"No Horizon, Legplates of the Crownless Host": "They outran the edge of the map and the edge of every blade; slip one and the storm you spin in that instant cuts harder for it.",
	"No Release, Boots of the Bound Dead": "The dead bound to them were never released and neither is he; near the end they hold him upright, and each blow that finds him is billed back to the striker in blood.",
	"No Retreat, Steps of the Last Line": "The sabatons of a line that never once stepped back; fully set, the mere stance of the man in them rattles the ones who try him.",
	"Noonday, Lance of the Unshadowed": "At true noon a lance throws no shadow and neither does the hand behind it — faith and strength drawn to one point, and every fourth verdict runs clean through the accused into whatever hid at their back.",
	"Noonheart, Armor of First Light": "A noon that never sets is held at its heart; the Choir's song breaks upon it, and every note it swallows is kept as charge for first light.",
	"Noonwalker Greaves": "The noonwalker plants his feet at the top of the day, and stood deep enough in that light the Judgment's leap comes back to him early.",
	"Null Crown, Plate of the Silent Siege": "Forged for the siege where no horn was blown and no quarter asked; every spell that dies against it feeds the grim patience of the man beneath.",
	"Nullsilk Chausses": "Legs wrapped in nothing that can be pinned, so chains find no purchase — but a body half in the void mends slow, and its healing knows it.",
	"Nullsilk Claws": "The claw-tips are dipped in un-thread; what they rake loses a stitch of whatever kept magic out.",
	"Nullsilk Hood": "Woven so fine the light passes through untouched — and so does most of a spell aimed at the head beneath it.",
	"Nullward, Hands of the Crownless Host": "The gauntlets that dragged the Choir's shieldings apart by hand; a shield-bash from them rips a ward open wide enough to march through.",
	"Nullward, Helm of the Crownless Host": "Worn by the pikemen who stood in the Choir's fire and made the burning into fuel; every spell that dies on it only stokes the man harder.",
	"Nullward, Legplates of the Crownless Host": "The leg-harness that walked out of a binding-field still walking; every hex it shrugs loose only sets the wearer's stance harder.",
	"Oath Sigil|B-A": "A masterwork oath-sigil, the device cut deep enough that swearing on it still means something.",
	"Oath Sigil|D-C": "The kingdom's badge, sworn to and never formally released; the oath outlasted the throne that took it.",
	"Oath Sigil|F-E": "A stamped tin badge, the kingdom's mark half worn away, worth more to its bearer than its metal.",
	"Oath Sigil|S": "A sigil sworn before the first Crown-fall and never once forsworn; the vow in it has outlived the kings who heard it.",
	"Oathflail|B-A": "A fine flail whose head sings a bell-note on the telling blow, the chime carrying the censure further than the strike.",
	"Oathflail|D-C": "Standard oathflail of the order, its links stamped one word of the vow apiece, so the chain reads a sentence as it falls.",
	"Oathflail|F-E": "A weight on a rusted chain, and a paladin who wears a chain round his own heart notices the crude kinship with every swing.",
	"Oathflail|S": "The links have been sworn upon so long they no longer rattle; the head falls where the oath directs and answers to nothing else.",
	"Oathiron Gauntlets": "Oathiron across the knuckles that answers a blow with a blow, on the old templar principle that a struck man strikes back.",
	"Oathiron Greathelm": "Forged of oathiron, which does not so much stop a heavy blow as refuse to be impressed by it; the worst of the strike sheds off the brow.",
	"Oathiron Legguards": "Oathiron greaves that settle deeper with every blow they take, until the wearer is less a man standing than a post driven.",
	"Oathkeeper's Seal|B-A": "A masterwork seal that closes a promise past breaking, hoarding grace between battles that a lesser token would have spilled.",
	"Oathkeeper's Seal|D-C": "A standard oathkeeper's seal, pressed to hold a vow shut, so mending banked before a fight is still there when the fight comes.",
	"Oathkeeper's Seal|F-E": "A cheap wax seal for closing a promise; it holds imperfectly, but grace kept behind it leaks away the slower for it.",
	"Oathkeeper's Seal|S": "The seal of an oath that has never once come open; whatever grace is entrusted to it keeps, undiminished, for exactly the moment it is needed.",
	"Oblivion Veil, Chausses Beneath All Names": "What tries to root him closes on a leg only half here; the hold slides off short, and the void repays the insult with a little of the life it cost.",
	"Oblivion Veil, Claws Beneath All Names": "They do not cut flesh but unpick the seam that holds a thing together, and a Hex laid through them unravels the wards on everyone it curses.",
	"Oblivion Veil, Hood Beneath All Names": "It does not block magic so much as forget the spell was ever cast, and hands the warlock back a little life for each casting it un-remembers.",
	"Oldhide Bracers": "Thick old hide that lends a war-draw the weight of the whole body behind it.",
	"Oldhide Hood": "Cured hide gone stiff with age, holding warmth the way an old scar holds a story — a little kept back against the cold to come.",
	"Oldhide Leggings": "Old pelt bound thigh to ankle; it will see you through a wound that ought to fell you, but a body this armored forgets how to catch a second wind.",
	"Onyx|Lv1-3": "A rough black nub, dense in the hand out of all proportion to its size, and it meets a knock the way stone meets rain.",
	"Onyx|Lv10": "Perfected to a mirror-dark boss of a stone; a full swing lands against it, spends itself, and the wearer walks on.",
	"Onyx|Lv4-6": "Polished to a working sheen, it takes a blow the way an anvil takes a hammer — by declining to notice.",
	"Onyx|Lv7-9": "Black and near-flawless, so heavy the plate around it seems to thicken, and edges skate off where they used to bite.",
	"Opal|Lv1-3": "A rough opal with a single flicker buried in the milk of it; those who fight in chains say it helps one motion remember the next.",
	"Opal|Lv10": "Perfected, no two glances catching the same fire twice; the wearer stops stringing blows together and simply becomes the string, one unbroken motion end to end.",
	"Opal|Lv4-6": "Cut and polished, and now a dozen fires shift across it — a stone for those who cannot afford to let a rhythm break.",
	"Opal|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless, alive with colour that never repeats, and the bearer's blows fall into a cadence of their own accord.",
	"Orbitless, Steps Between Stars": "She walks a path no star keeps, arriving before the fight expects her, and her opening spell lands already knowing where the ward is thin.",
	"Ossuary King, Chausses Beneath All Names": "The buried king will not kneel and will not let the man wearing his legs kneel; fully braced, the graveyard pays a third of the Pact's blood so the warlock keeps his own.",
	"Ossuary King, Claws Beneath All Names": "The buried king's own hands lent back for a war; whoever strikes them is answered and then held, slowed to the pace of the grave he'll shortly keep.",
	"Ossuary King, Hood Beneath All Names": "Crowned from the vault of every king interred beneath the capital; the blow it blunts, it answers, and the striker walks off already rotting.",
	"Ossuary King, Mail of the First Grave": "Mail from the oldest grave under Crownfall, worn by the first man ever buried a king; struck enough it stops giving ground, and the next to strike it rots for the presumption.",
	"Pactshield Codex|B-A": "A masterwork codex whose boards turn a blow and whose pages log the striker for later collection.",
	"Pactshield Codex|D-C": "A guildsman's pactshield codex, clasped and warded, keeping its bearer solvent enough to keep paying.",
	"Pactshield Codex|F-E": "A cheap chained ledger, its lock more rust than iron; it wards a little, mostly by being too heavy to swing past.",
	"Pactshield Codex|S": "A codex chained shut against what it witnessed; it keeps its bearer alive because the far side would rather he live to keep the debt current.",
	"Pale Bone Cowl": "Bleached bone-pale and cool to the brow; a little of every warmth you drink stays behind in it, kept for the next bad moment.",
	"Pale Bone Grips": "Bone-stiff at the knuckle; the weight behind each strike is a fraction more than the hand alone could give.",
	"Pale Bone Wraps": "Bone-splinted to hold the low ground: you take less when near death, and lean the less on ever vanishing at all.",
	"Pale Covenant": "A treaty signed in the ossuary between the living warlock and the patient dead: each blow he takes they take a share of, and stand a little firmer for it.",
	"Pale Flight, Blade Between Heartbeats": "It flies in the gap the heart forgets to fill; every blow that finds only air pays down the dark, and the next passage through it cuts deeper.",
	"Pale Guest, Light That Knows the Dead": "The pale guest carries a light that sees the already-dead — a dodge marks the attacker and holds a Death Mark's promise open.",
	"Pale Heel": "Pale at the heel from standing where the dying stand; near the end they hold the wearer a little longer above it.",
	"Pale March": "The dead do not hurry and cannot be stopped, so chains and frost slide off a stride borrowed from a column that never once broke ranks.",
	"Pale Web, Cloak Between Heartbeats": "The web takes the blow and gives back a roll; each thing that fails to land leaves a little stolen warmth in your veins for the trying.",
	"Pale Web, Cowl Between Heartbeats": "The web keeps what touches it; a dodge leaves the attacker exposed and holds a fading Death Mark open a breath longer.",
	"Pale Web, Grips Between Heartbeats": "The web guides the point — every fifth Stab cannot be dodged, and the warmth it draws lingers past its due.",
	"Pale Web, Wraps Between Heartbeats": "A blow throws you into the web's own roll; dash through the one who threw it and the slip renews.",
	"Parryshade": "The shade that stopped slipping and started catching — it takes the blade on the flat and hands it straight back.",
	"Patched Satchel": "More thread than leather now, mended by a dozen hands that each swore theirs would be the last patch.",
	"Piercer's Cleats|B-A": "Masterwork cleats that bite clean and let go clean, anchoring the shot and freeing the next step.",
	"Piercer's Cleats|D-C": "Issue piercer's cleats, spiked to hold the plant so the body's weight rides behind the string.",
	"Piercer's Cleats|F-E": "Bent nails driven through worn soles; they bite the ground, and the foot, in about equal measure.",
	"Piercer's Cleats|S": "Cleats that root the plant like driven stakes, so every arrow carries the full weight of the ground she stands on.",
	"Pike|B-A": "A master-hafted warpike, balanced so one man's reach becomes a wall's: the length of a soldier plus the length of his enemy's fear.",
	"Pike|D-C": "Issue infantry pike, hafted true and butt-spiked — the front rank's plain answer to anything that thinks charging is a plan.",
	"Pike|F-E": "A militia pike, more sharpened fence-rail than weapon; it keeps a wolf at arm's length and asks the man behind it to be brave.",
	"Pike|S": "A pike that has held a gate alone and won; nothing has crossed its point in living memory, and nothing intends to start.",
	"Pilgrim's Burden": "The longer road makes the heavier blow — judgment lands harder for the weight you carried to deliver it, and slower for the very same reason.",
	"Pilgrim's Resolve|B-A": "Masterwork pilgrim's resolve, sole and will alike unyielding, so a failing stand only sets the feet the more firmly.",
	"Pilgrim's Resolve|D-C": "Serviceable pilgrim's boots, cut for the long road, holding their footing the more grimly the harder the going gets.",
	"Pilgrim's Resolve|F-E": "Road-beaten boots patched a hundred times, worn thin but not through, and stubborn about whatever ground they finally reach.",
	"Pilgrim's Resolve|S": "Boots that have out-walked the shrines they set out for; whatever last ground they stand on, they hold it as if the road itself ended there.",
	"Pit Fury": "Brewed for the dogs that fight under Fangmoot Circle. Works fine on people.",
	"Pitted Iron": "Pig-iron with the slag still in it. Holds an edge for one good swing.",
	"Plain Bolt": "A clean bolt of undyed weave — the tailor's daily bread.",
	"Plain Ingot": "Honest soldier's steel — the metal the Ember Guard marched in.",
	"Poisoner's Vial|B-A": "A masterwork phial, the dose kept clear and potent, a conversation already half won.",
	"Poisoner's Vial|D-C": "A serviceable vial of the green, corked with wax against a careless pocket.",
	"Poisoner's Vial|F-E": "A chipped glass thumb-vial, half its dose crusted to the neck — still enough for one bad night.",
	"Poisoner's Vial|S": "A full vial of the perfected green; drawing the cork is the whole argument, and it is over.",
	"Poppyfield Haze": "Smells like a meadow you shouldn't sleep in.",
	"Potent Essence": "Distilled to a bright, biting clarity. The working alchemist's staple.",
	"Pristine Bloom": "Perfect, unblemished, glowing with its own faint life. A master's prize.",
	"Prowlers|B-A": "Masterwork prowlers that take a sheer face like a cat takes a fence.",
	"Prowlers|D-C": "Issue climbing boots, clawed at toe and heel for the wall the guards ignore.",
	"Prowlers|F-E": "Boots with cobbler's tacks driven through for grip — crude, but they hold a drainpipe.",
	"Prowlers|S": "Made for the roofline the assassin calls the real road; no wall is a wall to them.",
	"Pure Essence": "Not a mote of dross left in it. It glows faintly in the dark.",
	"Queen's Kiss, Vial of the Last Breath": "A kiss from the vial and the last breath is a formality; the green bores through any ward given time, and it is always given time.",
	"Quickmarch": "Light-soled marching boots; sidestep a blow in them and its owner is a beat slow closing back up.",
	"Quickweather": "The weather turns wherever she stands — step across the room and a small storm arrives a beat behind to finish what the first bolt began.",
	"Quiet Anvil Wraps": "Iron soles wrapped to a hush; the chains and frost that would hold another slide off the quiet weight.",
	"Quiet Ground, Greaves of the Unhexed": "Wherever these are planted the hexes go quiet; the earth that would have gripped a lesser man only steadies this one and stokes him.",
	"Quiet Passenger": "Something rides along in the fetish, quiet and attentive, and where it points the enemy's wards come a thread looser.",
	"Quietus, the King's Final Thought": "The thought a king does not get to finish; against the nearly-dead the needle does not wound, it concludes, and every ending it delivers whets the next Mark.",
	"Quintessence": "The fifth essence — what the old alchemists chased, and mostly died pretending to have found.",
	"Radiant Essence": "So concentrated it warms the vial. A master measures it by the single drop.",
	"Radiant Greaves|B-A": "Masterwork greaves that throw the light back defiantly, settling heavier and brighter the longer the wearer stands his patch.",
	"Radiant Greaves|D-C": "Standard radiant greaves that keep their gleam through any road, so the knight who holds his ground is marked holding it.",
	"Radiant Greaves|F-E": "Scuffed greaves buffed stubbornly bright, holding what shine they can on the notion that a planted knight should be seen.",
	"Radiant Greaves|S": "Greaves that shine like a fixed noon; wherever they plant becomes a spot the field cannot ignore and the wearer will not leave.",
	"Rainwall, Brigandine of the Last Volley": "Named for the wall of arrows that fell like rain on the last field the kingdom held; the more it is struck, the harder it stands, and it does not break.",
	"Ranger's Bracers|B-A": "Finely fitted guards that all but steady the hand themselves — the whole trade in the loosing wrist.",
	"Ranger's Bracers|D-C": "Ranger's issue bracers, fitted close so nothing snags on the way out.",
	"Ranger's Bracers|F-E": "Loose wraps that catch the string as often as they clear it; the loose is a gamble.",
	"Ranger's Bracers|S": "Bracers fitted so exactly that the string knows only one path off them, and it is always the true one.",
	"Ranger's Hood|B-A": "A finely tailored hood that swallows the face whole; you are a shape in the treeline and nothing more.",
	"Ranger's Hood|D-C": "A ranger's hood of standard cut, the cowl deep enough to be forgotten by anyone glancing past.",
	"Ranger's Hood|F-E": "A threadbare grey hood that hides a face only in poor light and worse weather.",
	"Ranger's Hood|S": "A hood cut for someone paid to see first and be seen never — under it she has watched armies pass an arm's length away.",
	"Ranger's Leathers|B-A": "Finely worked leathers that make no sound and cast no shine — armor whose whole argument is absence.",
	"Ranger's Leathers|D-C": "A ranger's issue leathers, dark and light, trading every ounce it can for silence.",
	"Ranger's Leathers|F-E": "Scavenged leathers gone soft and creaky, quiet only if you move like you mean it.",
	"Ranger's Leathers|S": "Leathers so silent and so dark she is simply not where the blow was aimed, and never was.",
	"Ranger's Leggings|B-A": "Finely tailored for the creep and the slip, so a step aside costs no sound and no balance.",
	"Ranger's Leggings|D-C": "Ranger's issue leggings, cut for the crouch and the sudden silent sidestep.",
	"Ranger's Leggings|F-E": "Thin worn breeches cut for the crouch, gone at the knee from too many creeping miles.",
	"Ranger's Leggings|S": "Cut so the body flows aside before the mind decides to; the blow lands where she was, which is nowhere.",
	"Rare Bloom": "Flowers a week a year in places the blight hasn't reached. Gather fast.",
	"Reaver Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork reaver's gauntlets, the spikes set true so a solid hit opens more than it closes.",
	"Reaver Gauntlets|D-C": "Spiked across the back of the hand; issued to no regiment, and kept by every soldier who outlived his.",
	"Reaver Gauntlets|F-E": "Spiked knuckle-guards lashed together from broken tools, as likely to catch on your own belt as an enemy.",
	"Reaver Gauntlets|S": "The gauntlets of a soldier who buried the men beside him and kept killing; the spikes carry a tally no one alive can finish reading.",
	"Reaver Helm|B-A": "A masterwork antlered greathelm, made to be the first thing an enemy sees and the last thing he forgets.",
	"Reaver Helm|D-C": "A scored reaver's helm worn low over the eyes by men who did the kingdom's ugly work and stopped pretending otherwise.",
	"Reaver Helm|F-E": "A crude horned cap, the horns lashed on with wire by someone who wanted to look worse than he was.",
	"Reaver Helm|S": "A helm that led every charge from the front and was never once found among the fallen; the enemy learned its silhouette and broke early.",
	"Reaver Legplates|B-A": "Masterwork reaver's legplates, built to drive a charge home and keep driving after it should have stalled.",
	"Reaver Legplates|D-C": "Scarred plate that has plainly been forward more often than back; the marks are all on the front.",
	"Reaver Legplates|F-E": "Battered leg-plates, all the dents on the front because the wearer never learned to back up.",
	"Reaver Legplates|S": "Leg-harness that advanced across a field that ate armies; it has no dents on the back because nothing behind it ever caught up.",
	"Reaver Treads|B-A": "Masterwork reaver's treads, gripped to turn a charge's momentum into ground taken and kept.",
	"Reaver Treads|D-C": "Studded to bite the earth and drive forward; the tread pattern is always facing the enemy.",
	"Reaver Treads|F-E": "Crude studded boots, the studs half-shed, the tread already wearing smooth.",
	"Reaver Treads|S": "The treads of a soldier who only ever moved toward the fighting; the earth still shows where they dug in and drove.",
	"Recurve|B-A": "A masterwork recurve whose limbs snap true and even, storing the whole gale of the draw in a short span.",
	"Recurve|D-C": "A properly recurved bow, its arms bent back to standard so a small frame casts like a larger one.",
	"Recurve|F-E": "A short stave crudely steamed into a curve that half-holds; it throws harder than it looks and stings the wrist for it.",
	"Recurve|S": "A recurve bent to the wild's own logic — a short bow that throws like the storm and returns to still the instant it is loosed.",
	"Red Antler Gauntlets": "Barbed across the fingers so a solid hit opens more than it closes; the wound keeps bleeding after the hand is gone.",
	"Red Antler Helm": "Crowned with a reaver's antlers and worn by men who liked to be seen coming; the first blow of any fight lands the hardest.",
	"Red Antler Legplates": "Built to lunge behind a shield-bash; commit forward in these and the follow-through lands with real weight.",
	"Red Arithmetic": "Three cuts light, and one to settle the account — the sum was always going to come due in red.",
	"Red Contract": "The terms are plain and the price steep: while the blood runs low it will not let him fall, but it drinks first and Soulthirst takes what is left.",
	"Red Doctrine": "Its doctrine is written in red on the wearer's own account: every strike leaves a martyr's brand smouldering in the wound.",
	"Red Fold": "A single red fold at the collar, kept for the first cut; the opening strike from beneath it lands harder.",
	"Red Fold Cowl": "Folded once in red, for the first cut: the opening Stab from under this hood bites deeper than any that follow.",
	"Red Fold Grips": "Red at the fingertips: what they cut keeps cutting, a thin bleed left behind in the wound.",
	"Red Fold Wraps": "Cut to spring forward: the strike that follows a Shadow Dash lands with the momentum still in it.",
	"Red Haste Knot": "A prayer-knot tied in red for the vows a knight has no time to say slowly; pull it, and the next opening shows itself.",
	"Red Horizon": "Named for the line dawn draws over a field of the fallen — every blade it dodges sharpens the next stroke into a certainty.",
	"Red Maw Harness": "Barbed at every strap; grapple the man wearing it and you come away leaving skin on the hooks.",
	"Red Quarry Bracers": "Stained dark along the fingers; the arrows they loose leave a wound that keeps opening after the shaft is spent.",
	"Red Quarry Hood": "Dyed with the blood of the season's first quarry; it teaches the eyes to weigh a target before the string is even touched.",
	"Red Quarry Leggings": "Marked with the season's first blood; roll into the killing distance and the shot that follows arrives with the momentum of the closing.",
	"Red Reliquary, Staff of the Last Pulse": "A reliquary holds a saint's relic; this one holds the last heartbeat of everyone he ever bled, and every spell spends a little of that hoarded pulse.",
	"Red Spurs": "Spurred for the charge; commit forward in these and the drive carries further than it has any right to.",
	"Red Witness": "A red thumbprint set in glass, the only witness to the first cut — and it makes that cut count.",
	"Redspur Cleats": "Red-spurred to break into a charge fast, so the opening blow arrives before the accused has finished getting up.",
	"Reliquary|B-A": "A finely-wrought reliquary, its founder's relic genuine and proven, and blows that fall near it seem to lose their conviction.",
	"Reliquary|D-C": "A standard reliquary of some founder's bone, carried to the field on the plain faith that a relic dulls the blow meant for its keeper.",
	"Reliquary|F-E": "A tin locket of ash that may be a founder's or may be a hearth's; the wearer's belief that it turns a blow does most of the work.",
	"Reliquary|S": "A reliquary holding a true founder's fingerbone, and against the faith sealed inside it the hardest strike arrives already half-spent.",
	"Returning Dead, Cowl Between Heartbeats": "Worn out of one grave already: what your blade drinks past full does not spill but banks, saved against the walk back.",
	"Returning Dead, Grips Between Heartbeats": "The dead grip harder — while stolen warmth still runs hot in you, every blow lands with a corpse's cold strength behind it.",
	"Returning Dead, Wraps Between Heartbeats": "At the edge of the grave the wraps hold you standing, and the stolen warmth simply refuses to run out.",
	"Rivetleaf Bracers": "Riveted leaf-iron at the wrist: a blade that finds the draw-arm is answered by a snap of the bow itself.",
	"Rivetleaf Hood": "Leaves of riveted iron overlap at the brow, so the first heavy blow to land there loses half its teeth.",
	"Rivetleaf Leggings": "Riveted plates down the thigh; every blow taken while planted settles the stance a little harder into the ground.",
	"Roadworn Promise": "A promise worn thin by the road but never through, holding the line where it would break — though a devotion stretched this far leaves the body's own mending at half.",
	"Rootbound Sandals|B-A": "A finely grown pair whose roots take to any ground, holding a battle-mage firmer than argument.",
	"Rootbound Sandals|D-C": "Standard rootbound sandals, grown through with living root that grips the earth and slows the pull of a fight.",
	"Rootbound Sandals|F-E": "Rough sandals laced with dead root that grips nothing, and trips her about as often as it holds.",
	"Rootbound Sandals|S": "Sandals rooted into the world itself; to move her, you would first have to move the earth she has grown into.",
	"Rose Chapel Plate": "Chased with the roses of a chapel long since lost to the blight, and its blessing still turns the worst of the hymnfire aside.",
	"Ruby|Lv1-3": "A rough red chip, barely faceted, but it already lends a blade a little more of the same blunt directness — no cleverness, just more hit.",
	"Ruby|Lv10": "A perfected drop of held blood that never dims — the plainest promise a stone can make, kept to the letter: your blow simply lands harder than the world expects.",
	"Ruby|Lv4-6": "Cut clean now and darkened to arterial red, it rides an edge that finds meat with less apology.",
	"Ruby|Lv7-9": "Nearly flawless, and the weapon it sits in seems to swing of its own accord toward the killing weight.",
	"Ruin's Testament, Chausses Beneath All Names": "The will of the ruined read from the ground up; a Hex cast off this footing deepens at double pace, the wither racing to reach the sentence's end.",
	"Ruin's Testament, Claws Beneath All Names": "The last testament of a ruined line, filed into the flesh it crits; on the already-cursed it reads twice as fast, the rot keeping its own grim time.",
	"Ruin's Testament, Hood Beneath All Names": "The last will of a fallen order, recited at every opening blow; the bolt that opens the fight also opens the rot.",
	"Ruinstep|B-A": "Masterwork ruinstep, each footfall opening a small fault the world hurries to forget.",
	"Ruinstep|D-C": "Serviceable ruinstep boots; wherever the sole comes down, the ground briefly doubts it was solid there.",
	"Ruinstep|F-E": "Cracked soles that leave the dirt uncertain it was ever firm, a shallow fault the size of a bootprint.",
	"Ruinstep|S": "Where he treads the crust remembers it was always going to break; every step is a fault line held one heartbeat from spreading.",
	"Ruinweave Chausses|B-A": "Masterwork chausses of ruined footing, so the earth fails behind every step and never once beneath it.",
	"Ruinweave Chausses|D-C": "Serviceable ruinweave chausses cut from broken ground, so each stride is a small collapse he simply walks out of.",
	"Ruinweave Chausses|F-E": "Greaves patched from broken ground, each stride a little collapse he stumbles clear of more than walks out.",
	"Ruinweave Chausses|S": "Cut from ground the god-kings broke; the world gives way where he has stood and holds firm only where he stands.",
	"Ruinweave Claws|B-A": "Masterwork claws stitched from a broken law, and whatever they curse is cursed on the edge of catastrophe.",
	"Ruinweave Claws|D-C": "Serviceable ruinweave claws, seams running like fault-lines, so a curse departs them halfway to collapse.",
	"Ruinweave Claws|F-E": "Frayed gloves whose seams split like small faults, so a curse leaves these fingers already cracking outward.",
	"Ruinweave Claws|S": "Every seam a fault in the world's own footing; a curse loosed from them arrives as ruin that only needs a reason to spread.",
	"Ruinweave Hood|B-A": "A masterwork hood whose weave is a whole repealed statute, so to look at it is to read a sentence someone tried to erase.",
	"Ruinweave Hood|D-C": "A serviceable ruinweave hood, its broken-law thread evenly set, marking the wearer to the world as an exception.",
	"Ruinweave Hood|F-E": "A hood pieced from the char of a burnt writ; every thread is a rule already broken, and it reads as a threat even folded.",
	"Ruinweave Hood|S": "Threads cut from a law the god-kings unmade by outliving it; the world reads the head beneath as a clause it can no longer enforce.",
	"Ruinweave|B-A": "A masterwork ruinweave whose every thread is a repealed clause, so he walks the world like a rule it forgot to enforce.",
	"Ruinweave|D-C": "A serviceable ruinweave vestment cut from a collapsed law, letting its wearer move as an exception to it.",
	"Ruinweave|F-E": "A vest stitched from the singed hem of a broken decree; wearing it, the warlock is a small standing violation.",
	"Ruinweave|S": "Cut whole from a statute the god-kings unmade; to wear it is to be, briefly, the thing the world's law can no longer touch.",
	"Runed Bone": "Already scored with an old ward-mark. Half the work is done, if you trust who did it.",
	"Runed Haversack": "Accord canvas stitched with a stay-shut ward, so the seam holds through a fall that would have spilled an ordinary bag down a ravine.",
	"Runeplate Circlet|B-A": "A fine circlet, its runes cut deep and clean, warding the mind without the old familiar throb.",
	"Runeplate Circlet|D-C": "A serviceable runeplate circlet, its graven wards holding steady, the faint ache beneath a sign they still work.",
	"Runeplate Circlet|F-E": "A crude band of scratched rune-plate, its wards half-worn, trading a headache for a little protection and calling it fair.",
	"Runeplate Circlet|S": "A circlet whose runes have been proven past argument; the wards hold at no cost, and the ache is a memory of lesser work.",
	"Runeplate Handwraps|B-A": "A masterwork pair of gauntlet-wraps, the runes seated flush, delivering an arcane snap with every closed fist.",
	"Runeplate Handwraps|D-C": "Serviceable rune-plated wraps that make a caster's precise gestures into something that can also break a nose.",
	"Runeplate Handwraps|F-E": "Crude rune-scratched knuckle-plates, loose enough to pinch, but they turn a gesture into a passable punch.",
	"Runeplate Handwraps|S": "Wraps that make her hands a verdict; the runes fire on contact, and contact is rarely her choice to give.",
	"Runeplate Robe|B-A": "A masterwork robe plated wrist to hem in fine rune, the wards legible and the protection real.",
	"Runeplate Robe|D-C": "A serviceable runeplate robe, its reading-plates graven true — half garment, half grimoire worn into the field.",
	"Runeplate Robe|F-E": "A patched robe with a few scratched rune-tiles sewn crooked at the breast, more superstition than shield.",
	"Runeplate Robe|S": "A robe whose every plate is a proven law; to strike her is to argue with the text, and lose.",
	"Runeplate Underleggings|B-A": "A masterwork set of rune-etched legguards, seated and balanced, rooting her stance against the pull of the fight.",
	"Runeplate Underleggings|D-C": "Serviceable runeplate greaves under the hem, for a caster who would rather be shoved than moved.",
	"Runeplate Underleggings|F-E": "Crude rune-scratched shin-guards, ill-fitting, that plant her about as well as standing still would.",
	"Runeplate Underleggings|S": "Legguards whose runes argue with momentum itself; the world may shove, but she decides whether she goes.",
	"Rusted Scrap": "Corroded plate pried off the fallen. It remembers being armor, barely.",
	"Sabatons of the Oath|B-A": "Masterwork sabatons, the whole oath graven around them, and no compulsion hauls the knight a step he did not swear to take.",
	"Sabatons of the Oath|D-C": "Standard oath-sabatons, each joint stamped a word of the vow, so chain and freeze move the wearer only where the oath allows.",
	"Sabatons of the Oath|F-E": "Rough iron shoes with a vow scratched across the toe; they hold poorly, yet a hex still finds them slow to drag.",
	"Sabatons of the Oath|S": "Sabatons that answer to the oath and nothing else; drag them with hex or chain and they go exactly as far as the vow, and no further.",
	"Sabatons|B-A": "Masterwork sabatons, weighted to root a stance a shove could not budge.",
	"Sabatons|D-C": "Plate boots wide in the sole and dead-flat, built to be set down once and not lifted until the fighting stops.",
	"Sabatons|F-E": "Flat scrap-plate boots, heavy and loud, better at standing than anything else.",
	"Sabatons|S": "The boots of a man who set his feet on a line and was still standing on it when the field went quiet.",
	"Saber|B-A": "A masterwork saber light enough to answer before the counter lands; the dueling edge of a man who never stands still.",
	"Saber|D-C": "Standard cavalry curve, quick where the line is slow, for the soldier who trusts his footwork over his shield.",
	"Saber|F-E": "A scavenged horseman's saber, its curve gone shallow from too many bad whetstones.",
	"Saber|S": "A saber that has cut its way out of encirclements it had no business surviving; it is gone before the field knows it was there.",
	"Safe Passage, Boots Beyond the Curse": "Blessed at a wayshrine that outlasted the priests who kept it; root and curse slide off, and shaking free of them hands back a roll.",
	"Saint's Window": "A shard of chapel glass from a saint's window, and the light still coming through it softens even the hardest blow.",
	"Saintbone": "A true saint's finger, they swear. It stays warm in a cold room.",
	"Saintglass Gauntlets": "Fingertips of saintglass that fray a warded soul thread by thread, the patient way doubt gets into a false faith.",
	"Saintglass Greathelm": "Its visor is set with a saint's window, and the hymnfire that would blind another man scatters into harmless colours across the glass.",
	"Saintglass Legguards": "Set with saintglass that stands the wearer firm against every chain and freeze — though a man rooted this hard is slower to be mended.",
	"Sanctified Bulwark|B-A": "A masterwork bulwark, so blessed and so thick that a failing line re-forms itself along whatever ground the wearer holds.",
	"Sanctified Bulwark|D-C": "A standard sanctified bulwark, consecrated and immense, meant less as armour than as a fortification a man can carry.",
	"Sanctified Bulwark|F-E": "A slab of holy-daubed iron strapped on like a portable wall, crushing to wear, but a squad has crouched behind it before now.",
	"Sanctified Bulwark|S": "Iron so consecrated men have sheltered behind it while a corpse still stood inside — a wall you happen also to be within, and it does not fall.",
	"Sanctified Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork sanctified plate, the grip strong enough to anchor a shield through a siege and never tire of it.",
	"Sanctified Gauntlets|D-C": "Standard sanctified gauntlets plated to the wrist, built to hold a line's load and never unclasp in surrender.",
	"Sanctified Gauntlets|F-E": "Heavy scrap-plate mitts blessed with oil, awkward and slow, but they will bear a wall's weight before they open.",
	"Sanctified Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets that have held the wall so long the fingers have forgotten how to let go, and would not if they could.",
	"Sanctified Greathelm|B-A": "A masterwork bulwark helm, anointed at every rivet, so heavy the neck beneath it has learned to be a pillar.",
	"Sanctified Greathelm|D-C": "A standard sanctified greathelm, thick as doctrine and near as immovable, meant to keep one head upright when the rest bow.",
	"Sanctified Greathelm|F-E": "A heavy scrap-iron helm daubed with holy oil, graceless and cumbersome, but the last thing on the line to go down.",
	"Sanctified Greathelm|S": "Iron so sanctified and so vast the head within is the last part of the wall to fall — and it has outlasted the walls it stood beside.",
	"Sanctified Legguards|B-A": "Masterwork sanctified plate, so counterbalanced the knight becomes the fixed point the whole line pivots upon.",
	"Sanctified Legguards|D-C": "Standard sanctified legguards, iron from hip to heel, the weight that keeps a wall standing when the line leans on it.",
	"Sanctified Legguards|F-E": "Heavy holy-daubed leg-iron, ungainly ballast, but it roots a man where a lighter set would be shoved off the line.",
	"Sanctified Legguards|S": "Legs of iron sanctified past moving; when the wall leans and every other man slides, these remain, and the wall remembers where it stood.",
	"Sapphire|Lv1-3": "A rough blue chip the old lapidaries claimed catches a sliver of the very hour it was broken from the seam; even crude, it shaves a hair off the wait between blows.",
	"Sapphire|Lv10": "Perfected, and the cutters' old story reads as plain truth: the stone has kept a whole stolen hour, and it spends that hour on you, one narrowed heartbeat at a time.",
	"Sapphire|Lv4-6": "Cut clean, and the pause the wearer used to spend recovering seems to belong to someone slower.",
	"Sapphire|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless, and time grows generous around it — cooldowns close before the hand has finished the last motion.",
	"Sawbones' Miracle": "A Sable Court miracle, stitched in a cellar. The thread pulls itself back out.",
	"Scarseal Rust": "Closes a wound the way the Choir closes a door: nothing in, nothing out.",
	"Scepter|B-A": "A masterwork scepter, its focus cut clean and true, so a single muttered word leaves it as a lance of fire.",
	"Scepter|D-C": "College issue: a turned rod with a seated focus-stone, reliable enough to drill a rune into a first-year until it holds.",
	"Scepter|F-E": "A scavenged focus-rod, cracked crystal lashed to a stick; it channels a spark, and singes the hand that holds it as often as the target.",
	"Scepter|S": "A scepter that no longer needs the word — thought alone brims at its tip, and the fire answers before she has finished deciding to cast.",
	"Sealhand Cowl": "Hooded in a sealhand's colours: the crushing blow still lands, but the cowl reads it coming and shears off half its weight.",
	"Sealhand Grips": "Knuckled to catch and return — a blow that lands earns a short blade back in the same breath.",
	"Sealhand Wraps": "Bound tight against the low sweep: each blow you weather sets the stance a little harder to move.",
	"Shadeweave Chausses|B-A": "Masterwork chausses of woven shade, so the leg a blow aims for was never quite standing where it seemed.",
	"Shadeweave Chausses|D-C": "Serviceable shadeweave chausses woven loose as smoke, making his footing a rumor and his step a denial.",
	"Shadeweave Chausses|F-E": "Smoke-loose wraps caught from a fading shadow, so his stance smears and his step half-denies itself.",
	"Shadeweave Chausses|S": "The lower shadow of a man long gone, still walking; his stance is a rumor even he cannot confirm.",
	"Shadeweave Claws|B-A": "Masterwork claws woven of shade, reaching through the dark to a throat before the throat knows it is found.",
	"Shadeweave Claws|D-C": "Serviceable shadeweave claws that let the hand pass where the light won't, closing on what the eye called empty.",
	"Shadeweave Claws|F-E": "Shadow-scraps bound to the hands, letting the fingers slip a little past where the light says they should stop.",
	"Shadeweave Claws|S": "Gloves of ownerless shadow that do their own reaching; they close on where a thing truly is, not where it stands.",
	"Shadeweave Hood|B-A": "A masterwork hood woven from a shadow that outlived its owner, and it wears the warlock's face like a borrowed one.",
	"Shadeweave Hood|D-C": "A serviceable shadeweave hood, its dark cured to hold, so the eyes beneath sit a hand's breadth from where they seem.",
	"Shadeweave Hood|F-E": "A cowl caught from a guttering shadow, the face inside flickering, never quite where the light left it.",
	"Shadeweave Hood|S": "The last shade of a caster long unmade, still refusing to disperse; whoever looks for the face beneath it looks a moment too early, always.",
	"Shadeweave Robe|B-A": "A masterwork robe of woven shade, and a sword that finds it finds nothing but the absence it was swung through.",
	"Shadeweave Robe|D-C": "A serviceable shadeweave robe, its own shadow, so a swung blade meets only the dark it was aimed at.",
	"Shadeweave Robe|F-E": "A threadbare mantle of caught shadow that casts none of its own, snagging blows a heartbeat after they should have landed.",
	"Shadeweave Robe|S": "A shadow that stayed after its wearer went; it is the robe now, and every blow aimed at the man lands in the dark he left behind.",
	"Shadow Without Owner, Chausses Beneath All Names": "The unclaimed shadow keeps the legs moving on its own account, so a Rift torn open mid-slip drags the world in with a hunger to match his evasion.",
	"Shadow Without Owner, Claws Beneath All Names": "An ownerless shadow does its own aiming; the bolt it guides home never misses and comes back wet, a little of the target's life returned to the hand.",
	"Shadow Without Owner, Hood Beneath All Names": "A shadow that outlived its man and took the hood for a new caster; each blow it slips buys every standing curse a little more time to deepen.",
	"Shadowstep Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork wraps of woven dark, so the step and the shadow trade places faster than the eye follows.",
	"Shadowstep Wraps|D-C": "Serviceable shadowstep wraps bound in shade, letting him arrive where his shadow already stood.",
	"Shadowstep Wraps|F-E": "Rag wrappings smeared with caught shade, so his shadow reaches the next spot a half-step before his feet do.",
	"Shadowstep Wraps|S": "Bound in a shadow that goes on ahead; he does not cross the ground so much as agree to be where his darkness already is.",
	"Shadowveil Cloak|B-A": "Masterwork shadowcloth that pools like spilled ink and pours off the shoulders in motion.",
	"Shadowveil Cloak|D-C": "A serviceable shadow-cloak that hangs quiet and shows a witness only a moving dark.",
	"Shadowveil Cloak|F-E": "A scavenged travel-cloak dyed dark, more patch than cloth, but it breaks a silhouette at dusk.",
	"Shadowveil Cloak|S": "So black it keeps no shape at all; the eye slides off it and finds no edge to name.",
	"Shadowveil Cowl|B-A": "Masterwork shadowcloth that pours the light off a face, leaving nothing to hang a name on.",
	"Shadowveil Cowl|D-C": "A proper veiled cowl, the weave close enough to drink the little light a face gives off.",
	"Shadowveil Cowl|F-E": "A dyed sackcloth hood, black gone grey — it hides a face well enough in a dark alley.",
	"Shadowveil Cowl|S": "Woven so dark it reads as a hole in the room; witnesses swear they saw a shadow and no one inside it.",
	"Shadowveil Grips|B-A": "Masterwork shadowleather, supple enough to feel a lock give without a sound.",
	"Shadowveil Grips|D-C": "Serviceable dark grips that leave little print and catch little light.",
	"Shadowveil Grips|F-E": "Cut-down work gloves dyed dark, worn thin at the fingertips but soft on a latch.",
	"Shadowveil Grips|S": "They leave no print and take no light; the hand inside them is only a rumour.",
	"Shadowveil Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork legwraps, close-bound and soundless across gravel or board.",
	"Shadowveil Wraps|D-C": "Serviceable shadow-wraps that carry a step you can watch and still not hear.",
	"Shadowveil Wraps|F-E": "Strips of dark cloth bound leg to knee, ragged but silent on a cellar stair.",
	"Shadowveil Wraps|S": "They move without a whisper; a floor of dry leaves keeps the wearer's secret.",
	"Shuriken|B-A": "Masterwork edges weighted to the grain — five that leave the hand as one and arrive apart.",
	"Shuriken|D-C": "A balanced set of issue stars, honest steel that flies where it is pointed.",
	"Shuriken|F-E": "Filed off a broken cartwheel, uneven and eager to tumble; thrown enough, one lands right.",
	"Shuriken|S": "Blades so true they whistle one note; the fan opens like a held breath and closes on a throat.",
	"Siegebough": "A stave thick as a siege-engine's arm; its volley doesn't stop at the first body, or the second, or bother to count at all.",
	"Sigil Sandals|B-A": "Masterwork sandals whose sigil seals clean with each step, laying a continuous warded path.",
	"Sigil Sandals|D-C": "Standard sigil sandals bound with a closed sigil, so every stride completes the same small circle of ward beneath her.",
	"Sigil Sandals|F-E": "Crude sandals with a half-drawn sigil scratched at the sole, closing its circle only when it feels like it.",
	"Sigil Sandals|S": "Sandals that write a perfect rune with every footfall; wherever she has walked is warded ground.",
	"Silk Vestments|B-A": "Masterwork vestments, the ward-weave dense and even, so hostile magic finds no loose thread to catch on.",
	"Silk Vestments|D-C": "Standard scholar's vestments, plain and ward-woven, stopping no blade and never asked to — only the spells.",
	"Silk Vestments|F-E": "A threadbare acolyte's robe, its wards worn to suggestion; it turns a cantrip and little more.",
	"Silk Vestments|S": "Vestments whose weave has become a single unbroken ward; spells slide off the cloth as if it were the mere idea of a robe.",
	"Silkneedle": "Fine enough to slip between the links of any mail once the wearer flinches — and it is very good at making them flinch first.",
	"Silkward Circlet|B-A": "A masterwork circlet of layered ward-silk, tuned so spellfire slides off the mind like water off glass.",
	"Silkward Circlet|D-C": "A standard ward-silk circlet, its threads spun to snag a hostile spell before it reaches the thoughts it was aimed at.",
	"Silkward Circlet|F-E": "A frayed strip of ward-silk knotted at the brow; it catches the smallest hex and lets the rest through to sting.",
	"Silkward Circlet|S": "A circlet whose silk seems to listen; a spell dies in the thread before it fully forms, and she never breaks her reading.",
	"Silkward Handwraps|B-A": "Masterwork wraps of tight ward-silk, unpicking a hostile spell at the fingertip before it can take hold.",
	"Silkward Handwraps|D-C": "Standard ward-silk handwraps, spun so a spell that seizes her hands finds only cold thread and comes undone.",
	"Silkward Handwraps|F-E": "Scavenged ward-silk wound loose to the wrist; it frays a grabbing hex and frays itself doing it.",
	"Silkward Handwraps|S": "Wraps whose thread answers magic with magic; whatever she touches, warded or not, quietly comes apart in her grip.",
	"Silkward Underleggings|B-A": "Masterwork underleggings of dense ward-silk, a second warded skin beneath the robe's first.",
	"Silkward Underleggings|D-C": "Standard ward-silk underleggings, worn against the skin, quietly turning aside the spells that slip past the robe.",
	"Silkward Underleggings|F-E": "A scavenged ward-silk underlayer, thin and patchy, catching only the hexes the robe already missed by luck.",
	"Silkward Underleggings|S": "Underleggings whose silk wards the parts of her a spell would think unguarded; nothing reaches her by the low road.",
	"Singularity, Orb of the Unsolved": "The one problem no archmage ever closed, sealed in glass; its unfinished intent bores through any ward given the seconds to work.",
	"Skirmisher's Boots|B-A": "Masterwork skirmisher's boots, cut for the sidestep and the silent approach in one.",
	"Skirmisher's Boots|D-C": "Light-soled and quiet, for the soldier whose orders end where his own judgment about running begins.",
	"Skirmisher's Boots|F-E": "Worn soft-soled shoes patched at the toe, quiet mostly because the leather's too thin to be loud.",
	"Skirmisher's Boots|S": "Boots the enemy never heard coming or going; the ground forgets them the instant they leave it.",
	"Skirmisher's Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork skirmisher's gauntlets, cut so close the blade feels like a longer finger; the precise stroke never wavers.",
	"Skirmisher's Gauntlets|D-C": "Close-fitted issue gloves that keep the grip honest for the one clean cut a lighter man is allowed.",
	"Skirmisher's Gauntlets|F-E": "Thin patched gloves with the fingertips worn through, kept for the feel more than the guard.",
	"Skirmisher's Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets that have loosed a thousand perfect cuts and fumbled none; the hand in them does not know how to slip.",
	"Skirmisher's Halfplate|B-A": "A masterwork halfplate, pared down to nothing that slows a pivot; armor for the man who intends to be somewhere else.",
	"Skirmisher's Halfplate|D-C": "Issue halfplate — half the plate and twice the speed, the compromise a soldier makes when the line has broken and only his feet will save him.",
	"Skirmisher's Halfplate|F-E": "Cut-down scavenged plate, straps mismatched, worn light because the rest of it was lost.",
	"Skirmisher's Halfplate|S": "The halfplate of a soldier the enemy never quite pinned; it has been everywhere on the field and hit almost nowhere.",
	"Skirmisher's Helm|B-A": "A masterwork skirmisher's helm, swept back for clear sight and quick reads; the blade you duck was always going to miss.",
	"Skirmisher's Helm|D-C": "An open-faced issue helm, cut for a soldier who intends to see the blow coming and not be standing there for it.",
	"Skirmisher's Helm|F-E": "A light open cap of boiled leather and thin plate, worn by men who couldn't afford to be seen.",
	"Skirmisher's Helm|S": "The helm of a soldier no swing ever caught; it reads a lunge before the arm has finished deciding on it.",
	"Skirmisher's Legplates|B-A": "Masterwork skirmisher's legplates, hinged for a step quicker than the eye that's tracking it.",
	"Skirmisher's Legplates|D-C": "Issue legplates cut away at the knee for the sidestep, the pivot, the half-yard that keeps a soldier breathing.",
	"Skirmisher's Legplates|F-E": "Cut-down leg-guards, the knee-plates missing, worn loose for a speed the rest of the kit can't keep.",
	"Skirmisher's Legplates|S": "Leg-harness that has never once been where the blow landed; the pivot in these is faster than the decision to make it.",
	"Skyline, the Arrow Before Dawn": "The arrow loosed while the horizon is still grey and the prey still thinks itself safe — the first shot of the day always lands true, and every kill buys the dawn back.",
	"Skyquill Circlet": "Feather-light at the brow; the strike she leans out of is left marked and open for whatever answer she has ready.",
	"Skyquill Handwraps": "Quill-light wraps that steady the gesture, so every so often the bolt they loose simply cannot miss.",
	"Skyquill Robe": "Feather-quilled and near weightless, it turns the odd landed blow into a step aside she never meant to take.",
	"Skyquill Underleggings": "So light the leg forgets them, they turn a landed blow into a sudden slip aside — though the featherweight cut trims what her frost gives back.",
	"Slipsteps|B-A": "Masterwork soft-soles that press a floorboard without waking it.",
	"Slipsteps|D-C": "Serviceable slippered boots that let a foot arrive a breath before its sound.",
	"Slipsteps|F-E": "Soft-soled scavenged shoes, split at the toe but quiet on a stone floor.",
	"Slipsteps|S": "The foot lands before the noise catches up; a floor will swear no one crossed it.",
	"Smuggler's Blue": "No stamp, no ledger, no waiting.",
	"Smuggler's Rust": "Rode the Emberward road under a forged wax seal. Heals slow, seals hard.",
	"Solarch Greaves": "Arched like a sun at its height, they settle heavier the longer the wearer holds his patch of ground.",
	"Soldier's Knapsack": "Standard column kit, sized for a week's rations and a spare edge; roomy, honest, and long out of issue since the Guard that carried it fell.",
	"Soul Fetish|B-A": "A masterwork fetish bound with a whole final breath, keeping something with him that death had marked for elsewhere.",
	"Soul Fetish|D-C": "A serviceable soul fetish of hair, tooth, and a caught last breath, holding a passenger against its leaving.",
	"Soul Fetish|F-E": "A crude knot of hair and a chipped tooth, keeping some small thing near him that ought to have moved on.",
	"Soul Fetish|S": "A knot holding a soul that was meant to pass and did not; it rides with him, patient, having already been where he is going.",
	"Soulstar, Fetish of the Last Breath": "It holds a last breath that was someone's, and it knows the living from within, so whatever it unpicks it unpicks like an old resident who still has a key.",
	"Spellbreak March": "Greaves shod against sorcery, so a cursed field is only ground to the man marching over it.",
	"Spellscar Cuirass": "A breastplate scored bright by the spellfire it kept turning aside; the man inside walked out of the Choir's light unburned.",
	"Spellscar Gauntlets": "Etched knuckles that catch on a sorcerer's wards and pick at the weave; every blow leaves the enemy's protection a thread looser.",
	"Spellscar Helm": "Scored and rescored by spellfire that never once reached the man beneath — the scarring is proof, not damage.",
	"Spellscar Legplates": "Warding-salted at the seams so a caster's chill can't take root — though the same salts sour any healer's touch.",
	"Spring Axiom": "A charm holding one rule of the green — that nothing is wasted; the harm she takes is banked for later, though it draws from the frost that mends her.",
	"Springcore Idol": "Heartwood carved while the sap still ran; it hoards a little of every gain against a hard hour, though a body so hoarding lets its second wind go thin.",
	"Springwake": "Its frost is only half the season — where the cold falls, green wakes underneath, and every enemy the nova catches feeds the roots that mend her.",
	"Stag-Eye Totem": "A stag's eye set in horn, forever watching; the first shot of the hunt lands truer for its gaze.",
	"Standard of the Crownless Host": "The colours of the army that fought on after the crown was gone; driven into the ground and held, its full weight staggers all who come against it.",
	"Starfocus|B-A": "A fine focus that gathers a whole quarter of the sky, its borrowed light concentrated to a killing point.",
	"Starfocus|D-C": "A serviceable star-lens, ground true enough to hold one constellation steady and loose a bolt where it is aimed.",
	"Starfocus|F-E": "A pitted glass bead that catches only the brightest star and spits its light back as a bolt that wanders where it likes.",
	"Starfocus|S": "A lens that has swallowed a dead star whole; it does not borrow the light, it owns it, and hands it back as she pleases.",
	"Starforged Ingot": "Metal that fell burning the night the first Crown broke. It has never once rusted.",
	"Starshard|B-A": "A fine shard that has never once warmed, its old star-cold sharpening every spell it rides.",
	"Starshard|D-C": "A serviceable starshard, still cold with the distance it crossed, lending a bolt some of that long killing patience.",
	"Starshard|F-E": "A dull chip of star-slag, cold to the touch and dim, keeping only a memory of the fall.",
	"Starshard|S": "A splinter of a star still burning somewhere far off; the cold in it is the patience of something that will outlast the fight.",
	"Starstep|B-A": "Masterwork boots that print clean points of light behind her, the path itself briefly a constellation.",
	"Starstep|D-C": "Standard starstep boots, each stride leaving a faint fading star, a habit of astromancers who watch the sky while they walk.",
	"Starstep|F-E": "Worn soles that leave a smudge of dull light on the stone, less a star than a scuff.",
	"Starstep|S": "Boots that walk her from star to star; the ground is an afterthought she touches only out of courtesy.",
	"Starwater": "Water that remembers a sky before the first fall — or so its unknown maker claimed. Kesh keeps a standing offer for a sealed vial. Synthesis, she says. Something greater.",
	"Starweave Circlet|B-A": "A masterwork circlet threaded with a whole constellation's light, the field laid out to her early and whole.",
	"Starweave Circlet|D-C": "A serviceable star-woven circlet, its thread of caught light letting her mark a battlefield the moment it opens.",
	"Starweave Circlet|F-E": "A tarnished band with a single caught spark of starlight, enough to read the near dark but not the far.",
	"Starweave Circlet|S": "A circlet that reads the sky and the room as one page; she sees the fight's shape before the fight knows it has one.",
	"Starweave Handwraps|B-A": "Masterwork wraps threaded with living starlight, each gesture drawing a bright line the eye follows too late.",
	"Starweave Handwraps|D-C": "Serviceable star-woven wraps trailing a faint glimmer, as if the spell were already written on the air.",
	"Starweave Handwraps|F-E": "Dim star-thread wraps that leave only a weak smear of light, pretty and little else.",
	"Starweave Handwraps|S": "Wraps that write in the light of stars; the spell is finished in the air before her hands complete the shape.",
	"Starweave Robe|B-A": "A masterwork robe sewn with the light of a whole set constellation, cold and bright and unmistakable.",
	"Starweave Robe|D-C": "A serviceable star-robe threaded with dead-star light; in a dark room it is the only thing you see coming.",
	"Starweave Robe|F-E": "A dull weave with a few dying sparks in the thread, faintly luminous and mostly for show.",
	"Starweave Robe|S": "A robe wearing the last light of stars long dead; by the time you have seen it, the spell has already landed.",
	"Starweave Underleggings|B-A": "Masterwork leggings threaded with caught light, planting her firm for the heaviest opening she can throw.",
	"Starweave Underleggings|D-C": "Serviceable star-woven underleggings, steadying the stance for the long opening cast when the first spell must be loudest.",
	"Starweave Underleggings|F-E": "Dull star-thread leg-wraps that steady the knees a little and glimmer even less.",
	"Starweave Underleggings|S": "Underleggings that hold her still as a fixed star; the opening cast leaves her without a tremor to spoil it.",
	"Stiletto|B-A": "A masterwork needle ground to a whisper of steel, parting mail at the seam without a sound.",
	"Stiletto|D-C": "An issue poniard, honest and narrow; it slides between two ribs and asks no questions.",
	"Stiletto|F-E": "A sharpened tent-nail with a rag for a grip — it finds the gap because it is too crude to do anything else.",
	"Stiletto|S": "A blade the eye loses edge-on — it is between the ribs before the mark thinks to flinch.",
	"Stillpoint, Boots Beneath the World": "Set so deep they seem to reach the still point under everything; at the very edge of falling, the world itself refuses to let the wearer go down.",
	"Stone's Refusal": "Plate that simply declines to yield — each blow it soaks sets it harder against the next.",
	"Stonebark Ward|B-A": "A finely kept ward of ancient bark, patient and unmoving, that soaks a share of what is thrown at you.",
	"Stonebark Ward|D-C": "A serviceable stonebark ward, hardwood standing between you and harm as it stood between grove and winter.",
	"Stonebark Ward|F-E": "A curl of old bark on a cord that keeps off a little harm, or a little chill — hard to say which.",
	"Stonebark Ward|S": "Bark from a tree old enough to have watched the kingdom rise; it guards her the way it guarded a grove for a hundred winters.",
	"Stonefather, Hands of the Crownless Host": "Fists like foundation-stones; in full fury the whole mass of you rides down the arm and into every strike, doubled.",
	"Stonefather, Helm of the Crownless Host": "The oldest helm in the muster, handed father to son past counting; in a killing rage the blood you drink over-full sets into stone around you, twice as fast.",
	"Stonefather, Legplates of the Crownless Host": "The stance of a man who has decided the ground under him is the last of the kingdom; the closer to falling, the higher the wall he can raise.",
	"Stormglass": "Glass-light struck from the storms that unmade the Stormwarden line, taken neat. Its maker is unknown; modern alchemists theorize it can be synthesized into something greater.",
	"Stormneedle Bracers": "Threaded with the same storm-glass needles; every arrow off them leaves a fray in whatever ward it grazes.",
	"Stormneedle Hood": "Stitched with needles of hardened storm-glass that drink a little of any spellfire before it reaches the eyes.",
	"Stormneedle Leggings": "Storm-glass sewn down the shins turns aside frost and snare — but the cold it locks out is the cold that closes wounds, so you mend slower for standing so sure.",
	"Stormpinion": "A stormbird's flight-feather; a heavy blow that lands near it loses some of its weight to the wind.",
	"Stormsick Blue": "The storm arrives all at once. So does the bill.",
	"Stormweave Bracers|B-A": "Finely woven guards that keep the string true off the sleeve no matter the weather.",
	"Stormweave Bracers|D-C": "Serviceable stormweave bracers; a clean release, rain or shine, which wins more hunts than a good arrow.",
	"Stormweave Bracers|F-E": "Frayed cloth wraps that keep the string off a wet sleeve about half the time.",
	"Stormweave Bracers|S": "Bracers that make weather irrelevant to the loose; the storm may rage, the release does not care.",
	"Stormweave Hood|B-A": "Finely woven against sky and squall alike, so nothing the weather does ever reaches the shot.",
	"Stormweave Hood|D-C": "A serviceable stormweave hood; the weather runs off it and the wind reads as a whisper, mostly.",
	"Stormweave Hood|F-E": "A patched oilcloth hood that keeps most of the rain out of one eye at a time.",
	"Stormweave Hood|S": "Woven so close the storm itself seems to agree to stay outside; she has drawn in a downpour and never felt it.",
	"Stormweave Jerkin|B-A": "Finely layered against sky and cold, so no hour spent still ever stiffens the draw.",
	"Stormweave Jerkin|D-C": "An issue stormweave jerkin, cut to shed weather so the body stays loose for the long wait.",
	"Stormweave Jerkin|F-E": "A layered rag-jerkin that keeps the frost off and little else; better than dying of exposure, barely.",
	"Stormweave Jerkin|S": "Woven so the storm never touches skin — she has lain out a night of sleet and risen ready to loose at dawn.",
	"Stormweave Leggings|B-A": "Finely woven against wet and cold, so legs that stand an hour can still run the mile after.",
	"Stormweave Leggings|D-C": "Issue stormweave leggings that hold off frost and bog through the long still hour before the shot.",
	"Stormweave Leggings|F-E": "Patched wraps that keep the worst of the marsh off the shins and let the rest soak through.",
	"Stormweave Leggings|S": "Woven so the ground's own hazards let go; snowmelt, snare, and frost all slide from her the way water leaves oiled feathers.",
	"Studded Bracers|B-A": "Masterwork studding along the forearm that turns a slipped blade and answers it with the wrist behind the bow.",
	"Studded Bracers|D-C": "Issue studded bracers, iron at the guard so a stray edge meets the wrist and thinks twice.",
	"Studded Bracers|F-E": "Cracked wrist-leather with a stud or two; a slipped blade still gets through as often as not.",
	"Studded Bracers|S": "A guard of set iron that catches the blade meant for the draw-arm and makes the striker regret closing at all.",
	"Studded Brigandine|B-A": "Masterwork brigandine, every rivet true, that has caught more than one stroke meant to end the draw.",
	"Studded Brigandine|D-C": "A serviceable studded coat, a thousand nails between two hides catching what a light blade offers.",
	"Studded Brigandine|F-E": "A crude brigandine with rivets working loose; heavy on the bow-arm and only sometimes worth the weight.",
	"Studded Brigandine|S": "A wall of set iron worn like a second skin; the blade that reaches her string has yet to be forged.",
	"Studded Hood|B-A": "Masterwork studding across the crown, each rivet placed to shrug a blow without slowing the turn of a head.",
	"Studded Hood|D-C": "An issue studded hood, iron set even at the brow to catch a glancing edge.",
	"Studded Hood|F-E": "Cracked leather with a few rivets hammered in crooked; it turns a slap, not much more.",
	"Studded Hood|S": "A brow of overlapped iron leaves that has turned more than one killing stroke into a story told later.",
	"Studded Leggings|B-A": "Masterwork studding down the shin, set to root the stance harder the longer the line is held.",
	"Studded Leggings|D-C": "Serviceable studded leggings for the archer who plants and holds rather than dancing off the line.",
	"Studded Leggings|F-E": "Crude leg-wraps with a stud at the knee; enough to plant on rough ground, not enough to trust.",
	"Studded Leggings|S": "Legs plated to stand a charge unmoved; where she sets her feet becomes, for a while, a place the enemy cannot take.",
	"Sunburst Icon|B-A": "A masterwork icon of hammered gold whose light seems to catch on the enemy and go on burning after the blow.",
	"Sunburst Icon|D-C": "An issue sunburst icon, its rays beaten sharp, marking whoever crosses the wearer with a little of the noon's heat.",
	"Sunburst Icon|F-E": "A crude sun punched into a brass disc, worn over the heart to remind the enemy which side of noon he picked.",
	"Sunburst Icon|S": "A sunburst that carries a coal of the first noon; what it marks keeps burning as though the day would not let the sentence cool.",
	"Sunpetal": "A blossom that turns to face a sun it remembers from before the ash. Rarer than an honest king.",
	"Sunstone|Lv1-3": "A rough shard that holds a coal's worth of warmth against the palm; the Accord's cutters won't shape one under cloud, and already every wound the bearer deals runs a shade hotter.",
	"Sunstone|Lv10": "Perfected to a captured sunrise that never sets — the Accord swears only a handful were ever finished true, and each makes its bearer's every blow a small act of the burning that took the Crown.",
	"Sunstone|Lv4-6": "Faceted on a clear morning as the guild demands, it glows from within, and harm dealt through it carries a heat that lingers in the wound.",
	"Sunstone|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless, a splinter of trapped noon; the master who cuts one keeps the shutters open, and everything the bearer strikes burns a little on the way down.",
	"Swiftvow Cord|B-A": "A fine prayer-cord, each knot true, letting the knight say a dawn's worth of vows in the space of drawing a blade.",
	"Swiftvow Cord|D-C": "A serviceable swiftvow cord, its knots the daily oaths, kept close to hand so the renewal is never fumbled before the fight.",
	"Swiftvow Cord|F-E": "A frayed cord of counting-knots for the dawn vows, worn loose so cold fingers can find the next one fast.",
	"Swiftvow Cord|S": "A cord worn smooth by a lifetime of dawns; the vow is renewed before the thought of it finishes, and the opening it earns is always there.",
	"Swiftvow Gauntlets": "Sworn to a swift vow: hold the count, and the eighth judgment cannot be dodged, ducked, or argued down.",
	"Swiftvow Greathelm": "Cut for the quick vow: see the blow, slip it, and mark the fool who threw it that far open.",
	"Swiftvow Legguards": "The swift vow trades a measure of the Holy stance's mending for footwork; struck, the legs answer with a slip no healer taught them.",
	"Syrup Haze": "Thick as syrup, twice as sweet, half as sharp.",
	"Tanned Weave": "Hide worked soft or linen woven tight; either takes a stitch well.",
	"Tempered Steel": "Folded and quenched proper. A smith will nod at this.",
	"Tempest Crown, Bracers of the Last Gale": "The Stormwarden's own guards; the gale in them shreds a ward with a single shot, and her volley leaves every mark of it torn.",
	"Tempest Crown, Hood of the Last Gale": "Worn by the last Stormwarden who commanded the wind and was never commanded by it; magefire breaks on it like weather on a ridge, and her breathing never once caught.",
	"Tempest Crown, Leggings of the Last Gale": "The Stormwarden stood in the teeth of the gale and was not moved; ice and root slide off these, and shaking free of them hands you a roll back.",
	"Tempest Yew, Bow of the Last Gale": "Cut from the last yew on the storm-ridge, it swallowed a hundred years of wind that tried to break it — and looses that gale again in a fan of arrows with every fifth shot.",
	"Templar Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork templar steel, articulated so the closed fist strikes as one piece and turns a blade in the same motion.",
	"Templar Gauntlets|D-C": "Standard templar gauntlets plated to the second knuckle, drilled to keep the fist shut and the meaning plain.",
	"Templar Gauntlets|F-E": "Scrounged mail mittens stiff at the joints; crude, but a closed templar fist still argues better than an open hand.",
	"Templar Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets from a Guard that never unclenched; the fist they make speaks the whole of the order's mind and needs no other word.",
	"Templar Greathelm|B-A": "A masterwork templar greathelm, the visor fitted so close a blow finds no seam and the order finds no coward.",
	"Templar Greathelm|D-C": "Standard issue of the old Ember Guard, closed and faceless, built to make one knight indistinguishable from his order.",
	"Templar Greathelm|F-E": "A scavenged Guard helm with the crest snapped off, plain enough that no one asks which templar it outlived.",
	"Templar Greathelm|S": "The helm of a templar who never broke ranks in life or after it; behind that visor the man is gone and only the order remains.",
	"Templar Legguards|B-A": "Masterwork templar plates, weighted so the legs never tire of the march and never yield the line they were set upon.",
	"Templar Legguards|D-C": "Standard templar legguards, cut to march the column by day and hold the line by night as the old Guard demanded.",
	"Templar Legguards|F-E": "Mismatched leg-plates roped on for a march, graceless but game, the kind that hold a line long after the feet have stopped feeling it.",
	"Templar Legguards|S": "Legs the Guard would have measured a knight by; they have marched a kingdom's length and not once given the ground they stopped on.",
	"Templar Plate|B-A": "A masterwork suit of templar plate, every lame fitted so the knight settles heavier the harder he is pushed.",
	"Templar Plate|D-C": "Standard templar plate of the old Guard, recognisable across a field and drilled never to give the ground it stands on.",
	"Templar Plate|F-E": "A muster of mismatched Guard plates roped together; it will not be shifted off its ground, mostly because it can barely move.",
	"Templar Plate|S": "Plate a whole line once dressed itself upon; where this templar plants, the battlefield agrees the wall now runs.",
	"Tenacity|Lv1-3": "A rough grey fleck the earth never grew — pressure made it, and even crude it passes a little of that hard-won refusal to break along to the wearer.",
	"Tenacity|Lv10": "Perfected, a fleck of the deep that outlasted the mountain above it; the blight breaks the Vale around the wearer and finds them, blow after blow, simply still standing.",
	"Tenacity|Lv4-6": "Cut from stone that survived being crushed and came out denser, it dulls the weight of every blow that lands.",
	"Tenacity|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless and heavy beyond reason, it teaches the body its own history: to be pressed by the whole falling world and hold anyway.",
	"The Armor That Bites Back": "They stopped closing on the soldier in this — the harness tears what touches it, and the wound rocks the fool back on his heels.",
	"The Bastion's Answer": "The bastion keeps accounts: every wound you take is entered as holy charge, and your next Judgment spends the ledger back into the sinner who opened it.",
	"The Book That Remembers You": "Open it once and it keeps your name forever; speak that name back and the void behind the page yawns wide, a maw that curses the whole room the wave washes over.",
	"The Cover Between Worlds": "Its two boards are the only wall between here and the far side; when a killing blow lands, the cover yawns and the void it held shut breathes out, hurling the room clear.",
	"The Gate That Walks": "They said the fortress could not march, so a swordsman decided to be the fortress — raise it and every blow that lands is answered and thrown back reeling.",
	"The Hand That Refused Death": "The first of your kind was killed once and simply declined; the blow that should end you finds you already elsewhere, blood full of borrowed warmth and the Mark set.",
	"The Name Beneath All Names": "Under every name a thing answers to lies the one it cannot deny; sidestep a blow close enough and the rod hears it, and to be named by it is to be already cursed.",
	"The Ninth Star, Unblinking": "Eight stars of the old wheel rise and set; the ninth never closes its eye, and on the ninth bolt neither does she.",
	"The Red Pennon": "The pike-banner of the last charge that still believed in the Crown; where its point plants, armor forgets how to hold.",
	"The Shadow That Remained": "When its wearer was unmade the shadow declined to go with him, and every blow it slides out from under feeds back into the curses he left standing.",
	"The White Hart's Last Breath": "Strung with sinew from the white hart no hunter ever touched; take the blow that should have ended you, and its last breath makes the next three shots impossible to miss.",
	"The Word That Outlived Kings": "An oath older than the throne it was sworn to; the sorcery that breaks against that word only hardens the man who still keeps it.",
	"Thesis Orb": "A student's focus-sphere holding one stubborn thesis; her spells carry a little of its argument, wearing at the enemy's wards.",
	"Thinner's Red": "Thins the blood on its way through. The trace of rot is the recipe, not a flaw.",
	"Thornbow|B-A": "Masterwork briar, every thorn set with purpose — it hurts to string, and hurts far worse to stand before.",
	"Thornbow|D-C": "A worked thornbow, its barbs filed to serviceable teeth that bite the draw-hand less than they used to.",
	"Thornbow|F-E": "Raw briar barely tamed to a stave; it still snags the sleeve and draws blood from the careless.",
	"Thornbow|S": "Living briar that never fully died in the cutting; it drinks a drop of the archer and pays it back to everything downrange.",
	"Thornshade Shroud": "Thorn and shade for the last ground: it holds you upright near death, though you slip the less for leaning on it.",
	"Thousand-Nail Vest": "A thousand nail-heads pinned between two hides; each blow it eats packs the whole rig a little more solid against the next.",
	"Throneless, Edge of the Last Host": "The sword of an army that outlived its throne — each true cut calls up the dead of the Last Host to swing a heartbeat after you.",
	"Titan Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork titan gauntlets, their mass balanced so the fist falls like a dropped anvil and the fingers still close on a hilt.",
	"Titan Gauntlets|D-C": "Slab-fingered plate that turns the hand into a maul — subtlety was never the point.",
	"Titan Gauntlets|F-E": "Slab-iron mitts so clumsy a man drops as much as he holds.",
	"Titan Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets that crush what they grip; the strongest men the muster ever raised wore them like their own bones.",
	"Titan Helm|B-A": "A masterwork titan's helm, its bulk balanced so the weight becomes stillness rather than strain.",
	"Titan Helm|D-C": "A weighty guard-helm that settles the neck like a keystone; standing in it all day is a discipline of its own.",
	"Titan Helm|F-E": "A too-heavy iron drum of a helm that leaves a man's neck aching by noon.",
	"Titan Helm|S": "The helm of a breed of soldier the muster no longer produces — worn the way a mountain wears its own summit, unmoved.",
	"Titan Legplates|B-A": "Masterwork titan legplates, so heavily braced the ground gives before the stance does.",
	"Titan Legplates|D-C": "Legs of a wall — planted in these, they say, a man could take a cavalry charge to the chest and simply not move.",
	"Titan Legplates|F-E": "Overweight greaves that plant a man well enough and tire him twice as fast.",
	"Titan Legplates|S": "The legs of a soldier who decided the earth beneath him was the last of the kingdom, and would not be moved off it living.",
	"Titanplate|B-A": "A masterwork titanplate, its enormous mass balanced so the weight guards instead of grinding.",
	"Titanplate|D-C": "A full harness weighed in stone rather than pounds; wearing it is a sentence, surviving in it is a career.",
	"Titanplate|F-E": "Ill-fitted heavy plate that weighs a man down without quite keeping him safe.",
	"Titanplate|S": "Plate a whole siege spent itself against; it was the wall when the wall had already fallen, and it did not fall with it.",
	"Topaz|Lv1-3": "A rough yellow chip with one accidental facet; hold it to the light and the eye begins noticing where things are thinnest.",
	"Topaz|Lv10": "Perfected to one unclouded point that never wavers — it does not help you aim so much as refuse to let you miss the weak place.",
	"Topaz|Lv4-6": "Cut to a proper point now, it teaches the hand to arrive exactly where the flaw is.",
	"Topaz|Lv7-9": "Faceted to a single near-flawless edge of light, it finds the seam in anything and guides the strike straight into it.",
	"Trailboots|B-A": "Finely cobbled trailboots that have walked most of this kingdom and mean to walk the rest.",
	"Trailboots|D-C": "A wanderer's serviceable trailboots, made to measure a kingdom in days walked.",
	"Trailboots|F-E": "Broken-in boots resoled with whatever the road offered, and it offered little.",
	"Trailboots|S": "Boots worn to the shape of every road there is; whatever country the map ends in, they have a step ready for it.",
	"Truefoot, Stance of the Final Arrow": "The stance you take for the one arrow that must land; hold it long enough and the hunt's rhythm gathers under you, sure as the ground.",
	"Umbral Cord|B-A": "A masterwork cord of bound shadow, its knots holding fast whatever he has decided not to see.",
	"Umbral Cord|D-C": "A serviceable umbral cord, a length of tied dark knotted at each thing he refuses to let the light have.",
	"Umbral Cord|F-E": "A frayed length of tied dark, knotted here and there at whatever the wearer would keep out of the light.",
	"Umbral Cord|S": "A cord long enough to loop the moon and draw the dark across it, knotted at every truth he keeps buried.",
	"Unanswerable, Mantle of the Closed Door": "The door that gives no answer: stand behind it under fire long enough and the next fool to test it is left exposed for the reply.",
	"Unblinking, Plate of the Last Vigil": "The unblinking eye of the last vigil turns a blow it will not flinch from into empty air, and mends the watcher for holding the stare.",
	"Unbroken, Hands of the Crownless Host": "Hands that never once let go of the line; strike the man in these and the counter comes back hard enough to stagger the striker.",
	"Unbroken, Helm of the Crownless Host": "The helm of a man struck by everything and finished by nothing; the crushing blow lands flat, and its failure hardens him further.",
	"Unbroken, Legplates of the Crownless Host": "Legs that never gave a yard; braced to the fullest, the shield-bash they lend keeps the enemy on the ground that much longer.",
	"Unseen Road": "He walks a road no witness can follow; every blow it lets him step off of marks the striker and feeds a little more dread into every curse left hanging.",
	"Unsetting, Icon of the First Sun": "An icon of the first sun that has yet to learn how to set; its dawn-fire brands the guilty, and in Retribution it burns as though noon would never end.",
	"Unshadowed, Gauntlets of the Final Oath": "Nothing sanctified by these hands keeps its armour; where Consecration falls, the very ground begins unpicking the guilty.",
	"Unshadowed, Greathelm of the Final Oath": "No hex reaches the unshadowed brow, and every spell it drinks is filed away as holy charge — the Choir's own song, taxed to pay for its sentence.",
	"Unshadowed, Legguards of the Final Oath": "The unshadowed stride shrugs off the hex that would hold it, and renders the broken chain down into holy charge for the reckoning.",
	"Untouchable, Knot of the Empty Step": "Worn by the soldier no lunge ever caught; bait the blow and the storm you answer with comes round the sooner.",
	"Vampire Eye|Lv1-3": "A rough blood-dark chip the Hollow Choir's alchemists favour, still warm when it leaves the skin; gear set with it sips a little of what it spills.",
	"Vampire Eye|Lv10": "Perfected, dark as a closed eye and never once cold; the Choir cut only the finest of these, and it repays every drop the bearer draws with a swallow of the same.",
	"Vampire Eye|Lv4-6": "Cut into a smooth dark cabochon, it drinks back a fuller measure of every wound the bearer opens.",
	"Vampire Eye|Lv7-9": "Near-flawless and warm as a living thing, it turns spilled blood into the wearer's own, wound feeding hand.",
	"Vein Prison": "It jails the overspill of his healing against a harder hour — but the bars pinch the vein they are built from, and Soulthirst is left half a draught.",
	"Vein-Burn Blue": "It burns going down. That's the blightwater, and that's the point.",
	"Veinbound Chausses": "Bound so tight with vein-cord they hold his stance when the blood runs low — but the same binding pinches the flow, and Soulthirst gets half its due.",
	"Veinbound Claws": "Laced to the wrist with the wearer's own vein, so the more blood he carries the heavier every closing grip lands.",
	"Veinbound Hood": "Stitched to the scalp with a thread of the wearer's own vein, it saves whatever overspills his healing against the next hard hour.",
	"Veinroot": "The staff has taken root in the man who carries it and drinks upward, so his Pact hits harder for the extra measure of him that goes into it.",
	"Venomtread|B-A": "Masterwork treads, the green kept true in the heel and spent only when meant.",
	"Venomtread|D-C": "Serviceable venom-boots, a dose sealed in each heel for the stride to deliver.",
	"Venomtread|F-E": "Boots with a hollowed heel that leaks more of its dram than it keeps.",
	"Venomtread|S": "Every stride is half a delivery; the wearer walks poison into a room and out of it.",
	"Verdancy, Staff of the Worldroot": "Cut from the Worldroot that outlived the first fall, it teaches what old trees know: the deeper you are rooted, the larger the storm you can throw.",
	"Verdant Herb": "Grown deep where the soil never soured. Rich, green, potent.",
	"Verdant Shroud|B-A": "A masterwork shroud, green as still water and just as patient, that the eye passes over.",
	"Verdant Shroud|D-C": "A serviceable green shroud, cut to break a shape against leaf and bark.",
	"Verdant Shroud|F-E": "A moss-dyed cloak, patchy and damp, that lets a body lie unnoticed in the undergrowth.",
	"Verdant Shroud|S": "Green enough to become the grove itself; the wearer is another quiet thing the wood has swallowed.",
	"Vigil Gauntlets|B-A": "Fine watch-gloves, thin as intent, so the fingers find the perfect hold on the weapon without the eyes ever leaving the dark.",
	"Vigil Gauntlets|D-C": "Serviceable vigil gauntlets, supple enough that the hand reads the grip by touch and never breaks the watch to look.",
	"Vigil Gauntlets|F-E": "Cut-down leather gloves worn paper-thin, letting the watchman feel his hilt in the dark for want of anything better.",
	"Vigil Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets so trusted the hand forgets it wears them; on the longest watch the grip is simply there, sure and unseen.",
	"Vigil Greathelm|B-A": "A fine watch-helm, light enough to turn the head fast and wide enough to read a whole dark room in one glance.",
	"Vigil Greathelm|D-C": "A serviceable vigil helm, cut open at the eyes so the watchman marks the blow coming before it is his problem.",
	"Vigil Greathelm|F-E": "A light cap with the eye-slit hacked wider by hand, trading protection for the sight a night-watch cannot do without.",
	"Vigil Greathelm|S": "The helm of a watch that never blinked; it gives away the first blow to be certain of every blow after, and it is never surprised twice.",
	"Vigil Halfplate|B-A": "A masterwork vigil halfplate, cut away exactly where a watching man needs to twist and reinforced everywhere he does not.",
	"Vigil Halfplate|D-C": "A serviceable halfplate for the watch, trading the second layer of steel for the freedom to lean out of a blow he saw coming.",
	"Vigil Halfplate|F-E": "A single salvaged breastplate worn over rags, half-armoured by necessity, leaving the watchman all the room to duck he could want.",
	"Vigil Halfplate|S": "Plate pared to the sworn minimum by a watch that never lost a night; it gives up the armour it does not need to keep the man it cannot spare.",
	"Vigil Legguards|B-A": "Fine watch-greaves, balanced so a heavy man moves like a rumour and the dark never hears him coming.",
	"Vigil Legguards|D-C": "Serviceable vigil legguards, greaved lightly, so the watchman changes his footing without a sound the sleepers would catch.",
	"Vigil Legguards|F-E": "Thin leg-wraps kept deliberately light, letting the watch shift its feet in silence for lack of any quieter armour.",
	"Vigil Legguards|S": "Legguards of a watch that walked all night unheard; they carry an armoured knight past a sleeping ward without troubling either's rest.",
	"Vigil Steps|B-A": "Fine watch-boots, soundless on stone, letting a knight in full plate move like the hour before dawn.",
	"Vigil Steps|D-C": "Serviceable vigil steps, soft-soled, made to walk an armoured watchman past a dozing ward without rousing either.",
	"Vigil Steps|F-E": "Rag-wrapped soles for the watch, quiet by poverty, carrying a heavy man past a light sleeper on luck as much as craft.",
	"Vigil Steps|S": "Boots of a watch that was never heard to pass; they carry the heaviest man past the lightest sleeper, and the ward wakes to find the round already walked.",
	"Vigil Without End, Gauntlets of the Final Oath": "The unending vigil makes every fifth judgment a certainty, and every certainty mends the arbiter a little for the keeping of it.",
	"Vigil Without End, Greathelm of the Final Oath": "The vigil that never ends reads a blow before it lands; slip it, and the guard you raised holds a heartbeat longer for the trouble.",
	"Vigil Without End, Legguards of the Final Oath": "The endless vigil turns a taken blow into a sidestep, and a judgment leapt from that slipping footing lands with the whole watch's grievance behind it.",
	"Voidsilk Chausses|B-A": "Masterwork chausses that hold the legs half out of the world, so what reaches for them closes on almost nothing.",
	"Voidsilk Chausses|D-C": "Serviceable voidsilk chausses of not-quite-existing cloth, so his footing is a matter he could dispute.",
	"Voidsilk Chausses|F-E": "Threadbare void-cloth wound at the shins, leaving the ground he stands on faintly in doubt of itself.",
	"Voidsilk Chausses|S": "Legs wrapped in the world's own absence; the ground beneath him is a claim he never has to concede.",
	"Voidsilk Claws|B-A": "Masterwork claws sheathed in fine un-thread, unpicking a thing's wholeness with a touch too light to feel.",
	"Voidsilk Claws|D-C": "Serviceable voidsilk claws, evenly wound, so what they touch loses its grip on being whole for a moment.",
	"Voidsilk Claws|F-E": "Ragged void-thread bound over the fingertips; what they brush forgets, for a blink, how to hold together.",
	"Voidsilk Claws|S": "Fingertips clad in the world's own loose hem; where they rake, a thing simply comes undone at the seam that held it.",
	"Voidsilk Hood|B-A": "A masterwork hood spun where the void frays finest, so the head beneath it seems to sit in its own small night.",
	"Voidsilk Hood|D-C": "A serviceable voidsilk hood, evenly woven, that drinks the light off the wearer's face and gives none back.",
	"Voidsilk Hood|F-E": "A frayed scrap of void-thread stitched to a cowl; it swallows a candle's light and hands the room back a shade darker.",
	"Voidsilk Hood|S": "Woven from the loose thread of the world's own hem; light that falls on it does not darken so much as forget it was ever cast.",
	"Voidsilk Robe|B-A": "A masterwork robe woven of void-thread throughout, so a sword swung at his chest finds a place that has already left.",
	"Voidsilk Robe|D-C": "A serviceable voidsilk robe with no true inside, so strikes reach it and arrive somewhere that isn't quite there.",
	"Voidsilk Robe|F-E": "A patched voidcloth robe, thin at the seams, where a blow that lands sometimes arrives a beat late and half elsewhere.",
	"Voidsilk Robe|S": "A robe with no interior at all, only a hem and a hunger; blows that reach it arrive nowhere, and nowhere keeps them.",
	"Vowbound Sabatons": "Bound at every buckle by a spoken vow, they refuse to be dragged an inch the wearer did not choose to go.",
	"Vowspike": "Grip it and the vow re-orders the man: the prayer answers first and the arm learns to come second, faith made the sharper of the two.",
	"Wallcat Boots": "Clawed like a wall-cat cornered: strike the wearer and a blade comes back off the reflex.",
	"Warbanner|B-A": "A masterwork field standard, its device stitched to catch the wind and the eye at the back of a failing line.",
	"Warbanner|D-C": "A soldier's own colours, small enough to carry and heavy enough to rally to; men die slower where they can still see it flying.",
	"Warbanner|F-E": "A rag on a stick, its colours faded past reading, kept because a man has to rally to something.",
	"Warbanner|S": "The banner a broken company re-formed under after the crown was gone; where it stood, the retreat stopped.",
	"Warblade|B-A": "Masterwork field-steel, tempered to hold an edge through a whole line; the sword a veteran trusts because it has never once let go first.",
	"Warblade|D-C": "Regulation steel, issued by the thousand and buried by nearly as many; it does the kingdom's work and asks no questions.",
	"Warblade|F-E": "A conscript's broadsword, nicked and soft, reforged from something that used to be a plough.",
	"Warblade|S": "A broadsword that outlasted the regiment it was issued to — every notch a name, and it still cuts truer than the day it was drawn.",
	"Warbow|B-A": "Master-tillered heartwood that swallows the war-draw and gives it all back — poundage most archers can only lean into.",
	"Warbow|D-C": "An issue war-bow off the Ember Guard racks, seasoned enough to hold a full soldier's draw without complaint.",
	"Warbow|F-E": "A green-wood war-stave that groans at half draw; bend it too far and it is you who breaks first.",
	"Warbow|S": "A stave that answers a shield-wall the way a landslide answers a fence; the draw is a decision the whole body must agree to.",
	"Ward of Ash|B-A": "A masterwork ward pressed from the ashes of the first Crown-fall, holding its heat like a grudge.",
	"Ward of Ash|D-C": "A serviceable ward of ash, pressed from cinders, that remembers being flame and withholds a measure of the burn.",
	"Ward of Ash|F-E": "A pinch of char pressed into a clay disc, still faintly warm, keeping a little of some old fire held back.",
	"Ward of Ash|S": "The one law the first Crown-fall could not burn, rendered from its own ashes; it remembers the fire and refuses to give it all back.",
	"Warded Cowl|B-A": "A masterwork warded hood, its charm-thread proof against a caster's eye as much as a swordsman's.",
	"Warded Cowl|D-C": "An issue cowl, a hedge-seal stitched at the brow against sight and stray spellfire.",
	"Warded Cowl|F-E": "A hood with a luck-charm sewn crooked inside — the paranoid poisoner's first habit, and a cheap one.",
	"Warded Cowl|S": "Warded by a hand that outlived every rival; nothing looks in, nothing burns through, and the wearer dies old.",
	"Warded Fang|B-A": "A masterwork fang, spine hooked and hardened to bind another's steel and give it straight back.",
	"Warded Fang|D-C": "An issue main-gauche, its guard filed into a fang to catch a sweep meant for the throat.",
	"Warded Fang|F-E": "A notched cook's knife pressed into service to turn a blade — it works once, maybe twice.",
	"Warded Fang|S": "A fang that has caught more blades than it has drawn blood — the one dagger that welcomes the strike.",
	"Warded Grips|B-A": "Masterwork guarded gloves a poisoner trusts with the hands that mix the dose.",
	"Warded Grips|D-C": "Issue warded grips, reinforced across the back to catch a cut meant for the throat.",
	"Warded Grips|F-E": "Padded knuckles wrapped in charm-string — enough to turn one bad blade, if the wearer is lucky.",
	"Warded Grips|S": "Warded until no edge reaches the fingers; the hands that end others are the last to be harmed.",
	"Warded Mantle|B-A": "A masterwork mantle, its wards close enough that steel skids and spellsight slips past.",
	"Warded Mantle|D-C": "An issue warded mantle, charm-thread run through the lining against blade and eye alike.",
	"Warded Mantle|F-E": "A double-thick mantle with a stitched-in charm or two — crude insurance for a nervous trade.",
	"Warded Mantle|S": "Warded until nothing lands and nothing looks — the mantle of one who plans to reach the far door.",
	"Warded Wraps|B-A": "Masterwork legwraps, warded firm so no trip or bind takes the wearer off their feet.",
	"Warded Wraps|D-C": "Issue warded wraps, bound tight and charmed against the low sweep.",
	"Warded Wraps|F-E": "Shin-strips with a rough hex knotted at the ankle — you cannot poison what you cannot put down.",
	"Warded Wraps|S": "Hexed until nothing catches the ankle; the one who does not fall lives to pour the dose.",
	"Wardedsole|B-A": "Finely cut soles whose ward turns aside the clutching bog and the clutching hex alike.",
	"Wardedsole|D-C": "Serviceable warded soles, marked by a hedge-witch so snare and mire lose their grip on the ankle.",
	"Wardedsole|F-E": "Old soles scratched with a half-remembered charm that lets go of the bog perhaps one time in two.",
	"Wardedsole|S": "Soles ward-cut deep enough that neither the blighted ground nor what it hides can keep her feet a moment longer than she allows.",
	"Wardpiercer": "Ground to a single purpose — to find the seam in an enemy's ward and worry it wider, bolt after bolt, until the shielding is a formality.",
	"Wardsteel Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork ward-gauntlets, the salts worked into every joint; the grip stays sure through fire that would unstring a lesser hand.",
	"Wardsteel Gauntlets|D-C": "Warding-etched plate over the knuckles, so the hand that grips the blade doesn't go numb when a spell breaks against it.",
	"Wardsteel Gauntlets|F-E": "Chalk-rubbed scrap gauntlets that go cold and clumsy the moment real sorcery touches them.",
	"Wardsteel Gauntlets|S": "Gauntlets that dragged a burning man's sword-hand shut through the Choir's worst and never once let it open.",
	"Wardsteel Helm|B-A": "A masterwork ward-helm, its salts worked into the grain; the Choir's flame breaks against it and finds nothing to catch.",
	"Wardsteel Helm|D-C": "Warding-salted steel, so a hexcaster's fire slides off the brow instead of cooking the man inside.",
	"Wardsteel Helm|F-E": "A dented pot-helm rubbed with cheap warding-chalk that flakes off in the first rain.",
	"Wardsteel Helm|S": "A helm the Ember Guard wore into a wall of sorcery and out the far side — brow unblackened, the man beneath unafraid.",
	"Wardsteel Legplates|B-A": "Masterwork ward-legplates, the salts fused deep; a binding-hex slides off the steel and finds no purchase.",
	"Wardsteel Legplates|D-C": "Salted plate down to the greave-line, so a caster's chill can't root a man where he stands.",
	"Wardsteel Legplates|F-E": "Cheap salted greaves that flake their warding into a man's boots by the second march.",
	"Wardsteel Legplates|S": "Leg-harness that walked out of a frozen field still walking, while every man around it stood locked in the ice.",
	"Wardsteel Plate|B-A": "A masterwork wardsteel cuirass, sorcery-quenched clean through; spellfire washes over it and finds no seam.",
	"Wardsteel Plate|D-C": "A ward-tempered breastplate, worn by pikemen who had to stand and hold while the Hollow Choir's fire came down.",
	"Wardsteel Plate|F-E": "A patched cuirass smeared with warding-paste, more prayer than protection.",
	"Wardsteel Plate|S": "The plate of a man who walked into the Choir's light and out again unburned — the burning never reached the soldier inside.",
	"Wardstep Greaves|B-A": "Masterwork wardstep greaves, salted sole to shin; cursed earth is only earth to the boot that treads it.",
	"Wardstep Greaves|D-C": "Warding-shod greaves — the ground under a hexed field stops grabbing at the man who wears them.",
	"Wardstep Greaves|F-E": "Chalk-shod scrap greaves whose warding wears off before the first hexed field is crossed.",
	"Wardstep Greaves|S": "Greaves that walked a road the Choir had sown with binding-hexes and never once slowed to shake one loose.",
	"Wardstone Shoes|B-A": "A masterwork pair soled in solid wardstone, rooting her stance against all but the heaviest pull.",
	"Wardstone Shoes|D-C": "Serviceable wardstone shoes, their stone slivers planting a caster where a hex would rather drag her.",
	"Wardstone Shoes|F-E": "Chipped shoes soled with wardstone gravel, holding her against a light hex and slipping under a strong one.",
	"Wardstone Shoes|S": "Shoes whose wardstone refuses its ground to anyone else; nothing moves her a step she did not choose.",
	"Wardthread Circlet": "Its threads sip a measure of any spell that reaches the brow, so what should have scorched her mind arrives only as a chill.",
	"Wardthread Handwraps": "Ward-silk at the fingertips leaves a little of its unpicking in every spell she throws, fraying the enemy's own wards thread by thread.",
	"Wardthread Underleggings": "Ward-silk against the leg holds her rooted where a hex would drag her, though so much warding leaves little thread spare to mend her.",
	"Warhowl, Hands of the Crownless Host": "The reaver's own gauntlets, notched for every throat; spin these through a crowd and each critical bite tears twice as deep.",
	"Warhowl, Helm of the Crownless Host": "It led every charge with a howl in place of an order; the opening Cleave off the line folds a man's knees under him.",
	"Warhowl, Legplates of the Crownless Host": "The charging legs of the front rank; drive in and the whirlwind that follows hauls the whole field into reach to be cut down.",
	"Warmaul|B-A": "A masterwork maul weighted so the whole march behind a blow lands in one place, judgment made a physical fact.",
	"Warmaul|D-C": "An issue warmaul of the heavy line, slow to lift and slower to argue with once it is on its way down.",
	"Warmaul|F-E": "A quarry-sledge with a hasty grip, heavy past all finesse; it settles arguments by removing the man who was making them.",
	"Warmaul|S": "The kind of hammer that shattered a Crown thirty years gone; this one has smaller kingdoms in mind, and falls like the last oath.",
	"Warpath, Treads That Crossed the Dead": "They walked a road paved end to end with the fallen and never slowed; charge in them and the road opens again before you've caught your breath.",
	"Warpit Fury": "The pit-fighter's arithmetic: they die first.",
	"Watcher's Halfplate": "Half-plated for the long watch, it leaves a man the room to lean out of a blow he saw coming from his post.",
	"Wayfinder Treads": "Treads that have found a path through every kind of country; they carry you out of a killing wound, but a body always walking never stops to catch its wind.",
	"Weightless Iron, Soles That Made No Sound": "Iron that forgot both its weight and its noise: no hold keeps them, and shaking one loose opens the dark for another dash.",
	"Whisper Rod|B-A": "A masterwork rod that finds the true name beneath the spoken one and holds it fast for the turning.",
	"Whisper Rod|D-C": "A turned-bone rod tuned to the quiet under speech, steady enough to hold a name once it is found.",
	"Whisper Rod|F-E": "A hollowed reed that carries a whisper further than it ought; half the names it catches are wrong, and a wrong name still bites something.",
	"Whisper Rod|S": "Tuned to the silence that predates language, where a thing's oldest name waits; speak it, and the named cannot refuse to be cursed.",
	"White Theorem, Circlet Beyond the Firmament": "A proof so complete it files hostile magic under solved, and each spell it turns aside it quietly reclaims, mana for mana.",
	"White Theorem, Handwraps Beyond the Firmament": "The proof written by the hands that cast it: what her spells touch comes unwoven, and her frost tears open all it freezes at once.",
	"White Theorem, Underleggings Beyond the Firmament": "The stance the proof stands on: what would freeze her in place is halved, and shrugging it off returns a measure of spent mana.",
	"White Theorem, Vestments of Proof": "The finished proof worn as a garment: hostile magic breaks against it and pays back in mana exactly what it meant to burn.",
	"White Verdict": "A verdict handed down in white — no ink, no appeal — that begins unpicking a warded defence the moment it is read aloud.",
	"White Wind, Bracers of the Last Gale": "The high wind never wavers, and neither does the arrow it carries — the sure shot off these does not miss, and does not stop at one body.",
	"White Wind, Hood of the Last Gale": "The white wind is the one that runs before the killing frost, seen only by those who watch the treeline; dodge beneath it and the hunt's own rhythm quickens a beat.",
	"White Wind, Leggings of the Last Gale": "The white wind shifts the standing snow without seeming to touch it; a blow only slides you clear, and a roll taken so is a perfect one again.",
	"Whitefang Rig": "Strung with the white fangs of a pack's lead wolf; the first strike of any hunt lands harder for the teeth watching it.",
	"Whole Bone": "Unbroken and sound — the charm-maker's working stock.",
	"Widow's Compass": "Every blade it looses points the same way — the widow it makes is the one name you chose, and no other.",
	"Widowglass Veil": "Thin as widow's glass — a blow that should land throws the wearer into a slip instead of a wound.",
	"Wilted Sprig": "Half-dead already. Steeps into something bitter and weak.",
	"Windcut Gauntlets": "Close-fitted so the grip never wavers; every so often they loose a Cleave that simply cannot be turned aside.",
	"Windcut Helm": "Swept back so a dodging soldier reads the swing early; the blade he ducks leaves its owner wide open for the answer.",
	"Windcut Legplates": "Cut for the pivot, not the grind — a blow that grazes past kicks you into a duelist's roll, at the cost of standing-strength you were never meant to lean on.",
	"Windcut, Halfplate of the Uncaught": "Half-plate for the soldier the enemy never quite pinned; slip a blow in it and the shield comes back up to bash again a breath sooner.",
	"Windfeather Bracers": "Feather-light guards that seem to steady the hand on their own; now and again a shot leaves them that could not have gone anywhere but true.",
	"Windfeather Hood": "Trimmed with feathers that turn toward whatever moves; slip aside and your eye has already found the gap in their guard.",
	"Windfeather Leggings": "Cut so light a blow slides you a full step aside — but a body forever sidestepping never stands still long enough to catch its breath.",
	"Windfeather|B-A": "A finely kept flight-feather that names the wind exactly, so the arrow leaves already knowing its path.",
	"Windfeather|D-C": "A serviceable windfeather that never lies still, reading the breeze for the shot to come.",
	"Windfeather|F-E": "A gull's feather on a thong that tells you the wind is there, if not quite where it is going.",
	"Windfeather|S": "A feather that reads the high wind before it arrives, and lends a little of its weightlessness to a blow aimed at her.",
	"Windstriders|B-A": "Finely made and feather-light, so the wind seems to owe them a favor at every step.",
	"Windstriders|D-C": "Serviceable windstriders, light enough that the ground feels more like a suggestion than a claim.",
	"Windstriders|F-E": "Light scavenged shoes gone thin at the sole; fast, until a sharp stone reminds you they are nearly nothing.",
	"Windstriders|S": "Boots the ground barely notices; she crosses it like the last of a gust, there and gone before the eye settles.",
	"Winterking's Mantle": "Taken from the great white beast the northern tribes called the Winterking, who could not be run down and would not be brought low; at the edge of falling, his hide warms the wound closed.",
	"Worldbreak Treads": "Where they finally set, the crust gives; braced to the limit, the ground he has broken open breathes rot up into whatever dares close the distance.",
	"Worldmantle, Circlet Beyond the Firmament": "A shard of the world's own crust worn at the brow — what strikes her it keeps, and her frost pays into the same reserve.",
	"Worldmantle, Handwraps Beyond the Firmament": "Heavy as the world they are named for, their weight rides every spell she throws — and falls whole with the Meteor.",
	"Worldmantle, Underleggings Beyond the Firmament": "The world's own footing: near the end it will not let her fall, and every nova she casts on that last edge mends her twice over.",
	"Worldmantle, Vestment of the First Stone": "A vestment of the first stone laid into the world; near the end it refuses to break, and the clay itself closes her wounds as the blows keep coming.",
	"Worldroot Steps": "Grown through with a thread of the Worldroot: near the end it will not let her fall, and every blow that finds her there is turned to mana instead.",
	"Worldweight, Plate of the First Giant": "Said to be cut from the shoulder-plate of the first giant to hold up the sky; near death, the sheer weight of it turns each blow you take into iron in the marrow.",
	"Wraithbone Fetish|B-A": "A masterwork fetish, bound and kept so a stolen warmth has somewhere to gather.",
	"Wraithbone Fetish|D-C": "A serviceable wraithbone charm, a bone that still remembers the shape of a grip.",
	"Wraithbone Fetish|F-E": "A yellowed finger-bone on a thong, cool to the touch and quietly unpleasant to hold.",
	"Wraithbone Fetish|S": "A finger-bone that has never let go of one grip; carry it and every warmth you take finds a place to pool.",
	"Zealot Gauntlets|B-A": "Masterwork gauntlets fanged across the back of the hand, making every gesture of the zealot a small act of Retribution.",
	"Zealot Gauntlets|D-C": "Issue zealot gauntlets spiked at the knuckle, so the blessing lands whether or not the weapon came with it.",
	"Zealot Gauntlets|F-E": "Gloves with nails driven through the backs, brutal and unsubtle, so even a bare-fisted zealot delivers a little scripture.",
	"Zealot Gauntlets|S": "Hands that are weapons in their own right; the zealot need not remember the sword, for the blessing is already on his knuckles.",
	"Zealot Greathelm|B-A": "A masterwork greathelm forged full-face and forward-canted, built for the knight who leads with his head and his conviction both.",
	"Zealot Greathelm|D-C": "An issue zealot's helm, faceless by design, so the enemy has nothing to plead to and no one to plead with.",
	"Zealot Greathelm|F-E": "A bucket of a helm with the eye-holes bored crooked, no visor to lift and no patience to spare for it.",
	"Zealot Greathelm|S": "A helm with no opening to reason through; behind it the argument ended before it began, and the light comes on hard.",
	"Zealot Harness|B-A": "A masterwork harness of bare edges kept hot, its every plate a branding-iron pressed on whoever dares embrace the man inside.",
	"Zealot Harness|D-C": "An issue zealot harness studded with brands, so the enemy who closes takes a mark off the armour before the wearer lifts a hand.",
	"Zealot Harness|F-E": "A harness of jagged scrap and sharpened straps, ugly to wear and uglier to grapple — the zealot's penance and his weapon at once.",
	"Zealot Harness|S": "A harness that is all sentence and no shelter; to grip this zealot is to be branded with the judgment he came to deliver.",
	"Zealot Legguards|B-A": "Masterwork legguards sprung for the leap, storing the charge in the plate so the drive forward never falters.",
	"Zealot Legguards|D-C": "Issue zealot legguards, braced to plant and drive, cut for the knight who would rather advance than stand and doubt.",
	"Zealot Legguards|F-E": "Crude braced greaves lashed for the charge, all forward and no retreat, because the zealot fears stillness more than the blow.",
	"Zealot Legguards|S": "Legs that have forgotten how to stand still; the zealot in them is always a step into the enemy, and doubt never catches up.",
	"Zealot's Cleats|B-A": "Masterwork cleats that seize any footing and launch the charge clean, so the opener lands before the enemy sets his feet.",
	"Zealot's Cleats|D-C": "Issue zealot's cleats, studded to close the distance fast, because the first blow is the one this knight puts his faith in.",
